  // ── Event 140: The Farewell Sermon (632 CE) — Pattern C ─
  'j_m4_140': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_opening', x: 0.5, y: 0.6, icon: '📢', label: '"Hear Me Well"', labelAr: '"اسمعوا منّي"',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA began: "O people, hear me well, for I do not know whether I shall ever meet you at this place again after this year." The opening was a farewell, though many in the crowd did not yet understand it. He paused to let the words settle. Then he laid out the foundations of human rights, justice, and dignity that would echo for fourteen centuries: the sanctity of blood, the sanctity of property, the sanctity of honor.',
        fragmentAr: 'بدأ النبيّ \uFDFA: "أيّها الناس اسمعوا منّي فإنّي لا أدري لعلّي لا ألقاكم بعد عامي هذا في هذا الموقف." الافتتاحية كانت وداعًا وإن لم يفهمها كثيرون في الحشد بعد. توقّف ليترك الكلمات ترسخ. ثمّ أرسى أسس حقوق الإنسان والعدالة والكرامة التي سترنّ أربعة عشر قرنًا: حرمة الدم وحرمة المال وحرمة العرض.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #1739; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #1739; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_declarations', x: 0.22, y: 0.42, icon: '⚖️', label: 'The Rights of Humanity', labelAr: 'حقوق الإنسانية',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA declared: "Your blood, your property, and your honor are sacred to one another, as sacred as this day, in this month, in this city." He abolished all pre-Islamic practices of usury (riba): "All riba is abolished, beginning with that of my uncle Abbas." He abolished blood vengeance: "All blood debts of the Jahiliyyah are abolished, beginning with that of my family." He set the example by starting with his own family\'s claims.',
        fragmentAr: 'أعلن النبيّ \uFDFA: "إنّ دماءكم وأموالكم وأعراضكم حرام عليكم كحرمة يومكم هذا في شهركم هذا في بلدكم هذا." ألغى كلّ ممارسات الربا الجاهلية: "كلّ ربًا موضوع وأوّل ربًا أضعه ربا عمّي العباس." ألغى الثأر: "كلّ دمٍ في الجاهلية موضوع وأوّل دمٍ أضعه دم عائلتي." ضرب المثل بالبدء بمطالب عائلته.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih Muslim #1218; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih Muslim #1218; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36',
        didYouKnow: 'By abolishing his own family\'s financial claims and blood debts first, the Prophet \uFDFA established a principle that would define Islamic leadership: the leader sacrifices before the people. He did not ask others to give up what he was not willing to give up himself. Abbas\'s riba and his family\'s blood claims were the first to go.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'بإلغاء مطالب عائلته المالية وثأرها أوّلًا أرسى النبيّ \uFDFA مبدأً سيعرّف القيادة الإسلامية: القائد يضحّي قبل الناس. لم يطلب من الآخرين التخلّي عمّا لم يكن مستعدًّا للتخلّي عنه بنفسه. ربا العباس وديات عائلته كانت أوّل ما أُلغي.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'women_s_rights', x: 0.5, y: 0.25, icon: '👩', label: '"Treat Women Well"', labelAr: '"استوصوا بالنساء خيرًا"',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA said: "Treat women well, for they are your partners and helpers. You have rights over them and they have rights over you." He reminded men that women are a trust (amanah) from Allah and were married under Allah\'s word. He condemned violence against women and laid out mutual obligations. In a society where women had been treated as property, this public declaration — before a hundred thousand witnesses — was revolutionary.',
        fragmentAr: 'قال النبيّ \uFDFA: "استوصوا بالنساء خيرًا فإنّهنّ عوانٍ عندكم. لكم عليهنّ حقّ ولهنّ عليكم حقّ." ذكّر الرجال بأنّ النساء أمانة من الله وأنّهنّ تُزوَّجن بكلمة الله. أدان العنف ضدّ النساء وحدّد الالتزامات المتبادلة. في مجتمعٍ عُوملت فيه المرأة كملكية كان هذا الإعلان العلني أمام مئة ألف شاهد ثوريًّا.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sunan al-Tirmidhi #1163; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sunan al-Tirmidhi #1163; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_final_charge', x: 0.78, y: 0.42, icon: '📖', label: '"I Leave You Two Things"', labelAr: '"تركتُ فيكم شيئين"',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA concluded: "I have left among you that which, if you hold firmly to it, you shall never go astray: the Book of Allah." He then asked: "O people, have I conveyed the message?" The crowd roared: "Yes!" He raised his finger to the sky and said: "O Allah, bear witness. O Allah, bear witness. O Allah, bear witness." Three times. Then he fell silent. The last public sermon of the last prophet was over.',
        fragmentAr: 'اختتم النبيّ \uFDFA: "تركتُ فيكم ما إن تمسّكتم به لن تضلّوا بعدي أبدًا: كتاب الله." ثمّ سأل: "أيّها الناس هل بلّغت؟" هتف الحشد: "نعم!" فرفع إصبعه إلى السماء وقال: "اللهمّ اشهد. اللهمّ اشهد. اللهمّ اشهد." ثلاث مرّات. ثمّ سكت. انتهت آخر خطبة علنية لآخر نبيّ.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #1739; Sahih Muslim #1218',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #1739; Sahih Muslim #1218',
        didYouKnow: 'The Farewell Sermon is considered one of the most important speeches in human history. Seven centuries before the Magna Carta, fourteen centuries before the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, the Prophet \uFDFA declared the sanctity of life, property, and honor; abolished usury and blood vengeance; affirmed women\'s rights; and declared racial equality — all before over a hundred thousand witnesses.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'تُعتبر خطبة الوداع من أهمّ الخطب في تاريخ البشرية. قبل سبعة قرون من الماغنا كارتا وأربعة عشر قرنًا من الإعلان العالمي لحقوق الإنسان أعلن النبيّ \uFDFA حرمة الحياة والملكية والعرض وألغى الربا والثأر وأكّد حقوق المرأة وأعلن المساواة العرقية أمام أكثر من مئة ألف شاهد.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.78), Offset(0.5, 0.72), Offset(0.5, 0.6), Offset(0.36, 0.52), Offset(0.22, 0.42), Offset(0.28, 0.32), Offset(0.5, 0.25), Offset(0.72, 0.32), Offset(0.78, 0.42)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 141: "Today I Have Perfected Your Religion" (632 CE) — Pattern A ─
  'j_m4_141': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_verse_descends', x: 0.5, y: 0.62, icon: '📖', label: 'The Final Revelation', labelAr: 'الوحي الأخير',
        fragment: 'On the Day of Arafah, after the Farewell Sermon, the verse descended: "Today I have perfected for you your religion, completed My favor upon you, and have approved for you Islam as your religion." Five words in Arabic — "al-yawma akmaltu lakum dinakum" — and twenty-three years of prophethood reached their fulfillment. The religion was complete. Nothing needed to be added. Nothing needed to be removed. The message that began with "Read" ended with "I have perfected."',
        fragmentAr: 'في يوم عرفة بعد خطبة الوداع نزلت الآية: ﴿الْيَوْمَ أَكْمَلْتُ لَكُمْ دِينَكُمْ وَأَتْمَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ نِعْمَتِي وَرَضِيتُ لَكُمُ الْإِسْلَامَ دِينًا﴾. خمس كلمات بالعربية — "اليوم أكملت لكم دينكم" — وثلاث وعشرون سنة من النبوّة بلغت تمامها. الدين اكتمل. لا شيء يحتاج إضافة. لا شيء يحتاج حذف. الرسالة التي بدأت بـ"اقرأ" انتهت بـ"أكملت."',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Quran 5:3; Sahih al-Bukhari #45',
        sourceRefAr: 'Quran 5:3; Sahih al-Bukhari #45'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'umar_weeps', x: 0.25, y: 0.44, icon: '😢', label: 'The Tears of Understanding', labelAr: 'دموع الفهم',
        fragment: 'When Umar رضي الله عنه heard the verse, he wept. People asked why he was crying at a verse of completion and triumph. He said: "After perfection, there is nothing but decline." He understood what others had not yet grasped: if the religion is complete, the Prophet\'s \uFDFA role is fulfilled. And if his role is fulfilled, his departure is near. Umar\'s tears were the tears of a man who could read the writing on the wall.',
        fragmentAr: 'حين سمع عمر رضي الله عنه الآية بكى. سأله الناس لماذا يبكي عند آية الإكمال والنصر. فقال: "وما بعد الكمال إلّا النقص." فهم ما لم يُدركه الآخرون بعد: إن اكتمل الدين فقد تحقّقت مهمّة النبيّ \uFDFA. وإن تحقّقت مهمّته فرحيله قريب. دموع عمر كانت دموع رجلٍ يقرأ ما بين السطور.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #45; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #45; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36',
        didYouKnow: 'A Jewish scholar reportedly told Umar: "There is a verse in your Book which, if it had been revealed to us Jews, we would have taken the day of its revelation as a festival." Umar asked which verse. The scholar cited 5:3. Umar said: "I know the day and the place it was revealed — it was revealed to the Prophet \uFDFA at Arafah on a Friday, and both of those are already festivals for us."',
        didYouKnowAr: 'قال عالم يهودي لعمر: "في كتابكم آية لو نزلت علينا معشر اليهود لاتّخذنا يوم نزولها عيدًا." فسأله عمر أيّ آية. فذكر الآية الثالثة من المائدة. فقال عمر: "أعلم اليوم والمكان الذي نزلت فيه — نزلت على النبيّ \uFDFA في عرفة يوم جمعة، وكلاهما عيدٌ لنا."'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'what_perfected_means', x: 0.72, y: 0.42, icon: '✅', label: 'Complete and Sufficient', labelAr: 'كامل وكافٍ',
        fragment: 'The verse\'s implications are profound: Islam, as revealed to Muhammad \uFDFA, is complete. No prophet will come after to add to it. No scholar can subtract from it. The five pillars, the ethical framework, the legal principles, the spiritual practices — all are in place. The Quran is finished. The Sunnah is established. Future generations must interpret and apply, but the source material is sealed. "Today I have perfected your religion" is the lock on the revelation.',
        fragmentAr: 'دلالات الآية عميقة: الإسلام كما أُنزل على محمد \uFDFA مكتمل. لن يأتي نبيّ بعده ليضيف إليه. ولا يستطيع عالمٌ أن يحذف منه. الأركان الخمسة والإطار الأخلاقي والمبادئ القانونية والممارسات الروحية كلّها في مكانها. القرآن اكتمل. السنّة ترسّخت. الأجيال القادمة تفسّر وتطبّق لكنّ المادّة المصدرية مختومة. "اليوم أكملت لكم دينكم" هي القفل على الوحي.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Quran 5:3; scholarly consensus',
        sourceRefAr: 'Quran 5:3; scholarly consensus'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_weight_of_the_moment', x: 0.5, y: 0.28, icon: '⚖️', label: 'Between Triumph and Farewell', labelAr: 'بين النصر والوداع',
        fragment: 'This verse stood at the intersection of triumph and farewell. The religion was complete — triumph. The teacher was about to leave — farewell. The companions were standing on Arafah, the holiest spot on the holiest day, hearing the most final verse, from the last prophet, in the last sermon. Every layer of meaning reinforced the others. The perfection of the religion was also the beginning of the Prophet\'s departure.',
        fragmentAr: 'وقفت هذه الآية عند تقاطع النصر والوداع. الدين اكتمل — نصر. المعلّم على وشك الرحيل — وداع. الصحابة واقفون في عرفة أقدس بقعة في أقدس يوم يسمعون أكثر الآيات نهائية من آخر نبيّ في آخر خطبة. كلّ طبقة من المعنى تعزّز الأخرى. إكمال الدين كان أيضًا بداية رحيل النبيّ.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Quran 5:3; Sahih al-Bukhari #45',
        sourceRefAr: 'Quran 5:3; Sahih al-Bukhari #45',
        didYouKnow: 'The Prophet \uFDFA lived approximately eighty-one days after this verse was revealed. It was among the very last verses of the Quran to descend. Some scholars consider it the final verse regarding halal and haram. After it, the few remaining revelations were about the Prophet\'s personal situation and approaching death.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'عاش النبيّ \uFDFA نحو واحد وثمانين يومًا بعد نزول هذه الآية. كانت من آخر آيات القرآن نزولًا. يعتبرها بعض العلماء آخر آية في الحلال والحرام. بعدها كانت الوحي القليل المتبقّي عن وضع النبيّ الشخصي ورحيله القادم.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.8), Offset(0.51, 0.72), Offset(0.5, 0.62), Offset(0.38, 0.54), Offset(0.25, 0.44), Offset(0.45, 0.42), Offset(0.72, 0.42), Offset(0.63, 0.36), Offset(0.5, 0.28)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 142: Ghadir Khumm (632 CE) — Pattern B ─
  'j_m4_142': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_stop', x: 0.22, y: 0.52, icon: '⛲', label: 'A Halt on the Road', labelAr: 'توقّف في الطريق',
        fragment: 'On the 18th of Dhul Hijjah, nine days after the Farewell Sermon, the Prophet \uFDFA stopped the caravan at Ghadir Khumm, a watering hole where the roads to Medina, Egypt, and Iraq diverged. It was midday and intensely hot. The stop was unscheduled. The Prophet \uFDFA ordered those ahead to wait and those behind to catch up. He wanted the entire group present. Something needed to be said before the pilgrims dispersed to their different destinations.',
        fragmentAr: 'في الثامن عشر من ذي الحجّة بعد تسعة أيام من خطبة الوداع أوقف النبيّ \uFDFA القافلة عند غدير خُمّ منهل ماء حيث تتفرّع الطرق إلى المدينة ومصر والعراق. كان الظهر والحرّ شديدًا. التوقّف غير مقرّر. أمر النبيّ \uFDFA من تقدّم أن ينتظر ومن تأخّر أن يلحق. أراد المجموعة كلّها حاضرة. هناك شيء يجب قوله قبل أن يتفرّق الحجّاج إلى وجهاتهم المختلفة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih Muslim #2408; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih Muslim #2408; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_declaration', x: 0.42, y: 0.46, icon: '🤝', label: '"Whoever I Am His Mawla"', labelAr: '"من كنتُ مولاه"',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA ascended a makeshift pulpit of camel saddles and addressed the crowd. He reminded them of his authority and their obligations to Allah. Then he took Ali\'s hand, raised it, and said: "Whoever I am his mawla, then Ali is his mawla. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him, and be hostile to whoever is hostile to him." The word "mawla" in Arabic carries multiple meanings: master, friend, protector, supporter. Its precise intended meaning in this context has been a subject of scholarly discussion throughout Islamic history.',
        fragmentAr: 'صعد النبيّ \uFDFA منبرًا مؤقّتًا من أقتاب الإبل وخاطب الحشد. ذكّرهم بولايته والتزاماتهم تجاه الله. ثمّ أخذ بيد عليّ ورفعها وقال: "من كنتُ مولاه فهذا عليٌّ مولاه. اللهمّ والِ من والاه وعادِ من عاداه." كلمة "مولى" في العربية تحمل معاني متعدّدة: سيّد وصديق وحامٍ وناصر. معناها الدقيق في هذا السياق كان موضع نقاشٍ علمي عبر التاريخ الإسلامي.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih Muslim #2408; Sunan al-Tirmidhi #3713; Musnad Ahmad',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih Muslim #2408; Sunan al-Tirmidhi #3713; Musnad Ahmad'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_context', x: 0.62, y: 0.4, icon: '📖', label: 'Why This Declaration', labelAr: 'لماذا هذا الإعلان',
        fragment: 'Some scholars note that Ali had recently led a military expedition to Yemen, and some soldiers had complained about his leadership decisions upon return. The Prophet \uFDFA may have been publicly affirming Ali\'s authority and trustworthiness in response to these complaints. Others understand the declaration as having broader significance regarding Ali\'s status in the Muslim community. The hadith is authentic; the interpretation is where scholars have differed across centuries.',
        fragmentAr: 'يلاحظ بعض العلماء أنّ عليًّا كان قد قاد حملة عسكرية إلى اليمن مؤخّرًا وشكا بعض الجنود من قراراته القيادية لدى العودة. ربّما كان النبيّ \uFDFA يؤكّد علنًا سلطة عليّ وجدارته بالثقة ردًّا على هذه الشكاوى. ويفهم آخرون الإعلان بدلالةٍ أوسع تتعلّق بمكانة عليّ في المجتمع المسلم. الحديث صحيح والتفسير هو ما اختلف فيه العلماء عبر القرون.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih Muslim #2408; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih Muslim #2408; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36',
        didYouKnow: 'The hadith of Ghadir Khumm is narrated through multiple chains of transmission and is considered authentic by both Sunni and Shia scholars. It is one of the most widely transmitted hadith in Islamic literature. What differs is not whether the Prophet \uFDFA said these words — he did — but what precisely he meant by "mawla" in that context.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'حديث غدير خمّ مرويّ بسلاسل نقل متعدّدة ويُعتبر صحيحًا عند علماء السنّة والشيعة. وهو من أكثر الأحاديث تواترًا في الأدب الإسلامي. الاختلاف ليس في هل قال النبيّ \uFDFA هذه الكلمات — بل قالها — لكن في ماذا قصد بالضبط بكلمة "مولى" في ذلك السياق.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'ali_s_place', x: 0.8, y: 0.34, icon: '⭐', label: 'The Fourth Caliph', labelAr: 'الخليفة الرابع',
        fragment: 'Whatever the precise meaning of "mawla," Ali\'s رضي الله عنه place in Islamic history is beyond dispute. He was the Prophet\'s cousin and son-in-law. He was among the first to accept Islam. He slept in the Prophet\'s bed during the Hijrah. He carried the flag at Khaybar. He was father of Hasan and Husayn. He became the fourth Rightly-Guided Caliph. The Prophet \uFDFA loved him deeply, and the declaration at Ghadir Khumm, however interpreted, expressed that love publicly.',
        fragmentAr: 'أيًّا كان المعنى الدقيق لـ"مولى" فإنّ مكانة عليّ رضي الله عنه في التاريخ الإسلامي لا جدال فيها. كان ابن عمّ النبيّ وصهره. من أوائل المسلمين. نام في فراش النبيّ ليلة الهجرة. حمل الراية في خيبر. أبو الحسن والحسين. أصبح الخليفة الراشد الرابع. أحبّه النبيّ \uFDFA حبًّا عميقًا وإعلان غدير خمّ مهما فُسّر عبّر عن هذا الحبّ علنًا.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Multiple sahih sources; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum',
        sourceRefAr: 'Multiple sahih sources; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum',
        didYouKnow: 'After the Prophet\'s declaration, Umar رضي الله عنه congratulated Ali, saying: "Congratulations, O Ali! You have become the mawla of every believing man and woman." This narration, recorded in Musnad Ahmad, shows that the companions understood the declaration as significant and worthy of congratulation, regardless of the later scholarly debates about its precise implications.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'بعد إعلان النبيّ هنّأ عمر رضي الله عنه عليًّا قائلًا: "بخٍّ بخٍّ لك يا عليّ أصبحت مولى كلّ مؤمن ومؤمنة." هذه الرواية المسجّلة في مسند أحمد تُظهر أنّ الصحابة فهموا الإعلان على أنّه مهمّ وجدير بالتهنئة بغضّ النظر عن النقاشات العلمية اللاحقة حول دلالاته الدقيقة.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.12, 0.58), Offset(0.17, 0.55), Offset(0.22, 0.52), Offset(0.32, 0.49), Offset(0.42, 0.46), Offset(0.52, 0.43), Offset(0.62, 0.4), Offset(0.72, 0.37), Offset(0.8, 0.34)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 143: Return to Medina (632 CE) — Pattern D ─
  'j_m4_143': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'home', x: 0.5, y: 0.5, icon: '🏠', label: 'The City That Sheltered Him', labelAr: 'المدينة التي آوته',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA returned to Medina for the last time. This was the city that had taken him in when Mecca expelled him. The city of the Ansar, who had shared their homes, their wealth, and their lives. The city where the mosque was built, where the community was forged, where the state was born. He had conquered Mecca but never moved his capital. Medina was home.',
        fragmentAr: 'عاد النبيّ \uFDFA إلى المدينة للمرّة الأخيرة. هذه المدينة التي آوته حين طردته مكة. مدينة الأنصار الذين شاركوا بيوتهم وثرواتهم وحياتهم. المدينة التي بُني فيها المسجد وصُهر المجتمع ووُلدت الدولة. فتح مكة لكنّه لم ينقل عاصمته. المدينة هي البيت.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 36'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'visiting_the_graves', x: 0.25, y: 0.4, icon: '🪦', label: 'Baqi at Night', labelAr: 'البقيع ليلًا',
        fragment: 'In the weeks after his return, the Prophet \uFDFA visited the cemetery of al-Baqi at night. He stood among the graves of his companions and prayed for them: "Peace be upon you, people of the graves. You are ahead of us, and we are following." He visited the graves of the martyrs of Uhud. He prayed for Hamza. Those around him noticed the visits were becoming more frequent, more emotional. He was saying his farewells.',
        fragmentAr: 'في الأسابيع التالية لعودته زار النبيّ \uFDFA مقبرة البقيع ليلًا. وقف بين قبور صحابته ودعا لهم: "السلام عليكم أهل الديار، أنتم لنا سلف ونحن لكم تبع." زار قبور شهداء أُحد. دعا لحمزة. لاحظ من حوله أنّ الزيارات تزداد تكرارًا وعاطفية. كان يودّع.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih Muslim #974; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih Muslim #974; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'One night, the Prophet \uFDFA woke his servant Abu Muwayhiba and said: "I have been ordered to pray for the people of al-Baqi." They went together in the darkness. The Prophet \uFDFA prayed for the dead and said: "I have been given the choice between the treasures of this world and eternal life in it, or meeting my Lord and Paradise. I have chosen the meeting with my Lord."',
        didYouKnowAr: 'ليلةً أيقظ النبيّ \uFDFA خادمه أبا مويهبة وقال: "إنّي أُمرت أن أستغفر لأهل البقيع." ذهبا معًا في الظلام. دعا النبيّ \uFDFA للموتى وقال: "إنّي خُيّرت بين مفاتيح خزائن الدنيا والخلد فيها ثمّ الجنة وبين لقاء ربّي والجنة. فاخترت لقاء ربّي والجنة."'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_last_weeks', x: 0.75, y: 0.4, icon: '⏳', label: 'Time Running Out', labelAr: 'الوقت ينفد',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA spent his remaining weeks teaching, praying, and settling affairs. He freed his slaves. He paid his debts. He forgave those who had wronged him. He gave detailed instructions about governance. He spent more time with his family, especially Fatimah رضي الله عنها. He told her a secret that made her cry, then told her another that made her laugh. She later revealed: "He told me he was dying. Then he told me I would be the first of his family to follow him."',
        fragmentAr: 'أمضى النبيّ \uFDFA أسابيعه الأخيرة يعلّم ويصلّي ويسوّي شؤونه. أعتق عبيده. سدّد ديونه. سامح من أساء إليه. أعطى تعليمات مفصّلة عن الحكم. قضى وقتًا أكثر مع عائلته خاصّة فاطمة رضي الله عنها. أسرّ لها سرًّا أبكاها ثمّ أسرّ آخر أضحكها. كشفت لاحقًا: "أخبرني أنّه يموت. ثمّ أخبرني أنّي أوّل أهله لحاقًا به."',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3623; Sahih Muslim #2450',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3623; Sahih Muslim #2450'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'preparing_the_community', x: 0.5, y: 0.28, icon: '🏛️', label: 'Last Instructions', labelAr: 'تعليمات أخيرة',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA gave his final instructions to the community. He emphasized prayer repeatedly: "Prayer, prayer! And fear Allah regarding those your right hand possesses." He warned against taking graves as places of worship. He ordered Usama ibn Zayd\'s army to march north. He was ensuring that the community he had built would survive his departure — that the systems, the values, and the leaders were in place.',
        fragmentAr: 'أعطى النبيّ \uFDFA تعليماته الأخيرة للمجتمع. شدّد على الصلاة مرارًا: "الصلاة الصلاة! وما ملكت أيمانكم." حذّر من اتّخاذ القبور مساجد. أمر جيش أسامة بن زيد بالزحف شمالًا. كان يضمن أنّ المجتمع الذي بناه سينجو بعد رحيله وأنّ الأنظمة والقيم والقادة في مكانها.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #2916; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #2916; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'The Prophet \uFDFA also addressed the community saying: "Do not turn after me into disbelievers, striking each other\'s necks." This warning about internal conflict was tragically prescient. The fitna (civil wars) that would come within decades of his death were exactly what he feared most.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'خاطب النبيّ \uFDFA المجتمع أيضًا قائلًا: "لا ترجعوا بعدي كفّارًا يضرب بعضكم رقاب بعض." هذا التحذير من الصراع الداخلي كان نبوءة مأساوية. الفتنة (الحروب الأهلية) التي ستأتي خلال عقود من وفاته كانت بالضبط ما خشيه أكثر.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.78), Offset(0.5, 0.62), Offset(0.5, 0.5), Offset(0.36, 0.45), Offset(0.25, 0.4), Offset(0.5, 0.42), Offset(0.75, 0.4), Offset(0.62, 0.34), Offset(0.5, 0.28)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 144: Usama's Expedition Ordered (632 CE) — Pattern F ─
  'j_m4_144': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_appointment', x: 0.3, y: 0.55, icon: '👤', label: 'A Twenty-Year-Old Commander', labelAr: 'قائد في العشرين',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA appointed Usama ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه to lead a military expedition to the Byzantine frontier in Palestine, the same region where his father Zayd had been killed at the Battle of Mu\'tah. Usama was only twenty years old. Some senior companions, including men who had fought at Badr, were placed under his command. The appointment was deliberately provocative: it challenged both age bias and racial bias, as Usama\'s mother was Umm Ayman, an Abyssinian.',
        fragmentAr: 'عيّن النبيّ \uFDFA أسامة بن زيد رضي الله عنه قائدًا لحملة عسكرية إلى الحدود البيزنطية في فلسطين المنطقة ذاتها حيث قُتل أبوه زيد في معركة مؤتة. كان عمر أسامة عشرين سنة فقط. بعض كبار الصحابة بمن فيهم مقاتلو بدر وُضعوا تحت إمرته. كان التعيين استفزازيًّا عمدًا: تحدّى التحيّز للسنّ والعرق، إذ أمّ أسامة هي أمّ أيمن الحبشية.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37; Sahih al-Bukhari #4469',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37; Sahih al-Bukhari #4469'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_objections', x: 0.7, y: 0.55, icon: '🗣️', label: '"Too Young to Lead"', labelAr: '"أصغر من أن يقود"',
        fragment: 'Some companions questioned the appointment: how could a twenty-year-old lead veterans of Badr and Uhud? The Prophet \uFDFA heard the complaints and responded firmly from his sickbed: "If you criticize his leadership, you criticized his father\'s leadership before him. By Allah, his father was worthy of leadership, and so is he." The Prophet \uFDFA insisted that Usama\'s army march. Even as his health deteriorated, he kept asking: "Has Usama\'s army departed?"',
        fragmentAr: 'تساءل بعض الصحابة عن التعيين: كيف يقود شابٌّ في العشرين قدامى بدر وأُحد؟ سمع النبيّ \uFDFA الاعتراضات وردّ بحزمٍ من فراش مرضه: "إن تطعنوا في إمارته فقد طعنتم في إمارة أبيه من قبل. وايم الله لقد كان خليقًا للإمارة وإنّ هذا لخليق لها." أصرّ النبيّ \uFDFA على أن يزحف جيش أسامة. حتّى مع تدهور صحّته ظلّ يسأل: "أنفذوا جيش أسامة؟"',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4469; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4469; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'Usama\'s army eventually marched after the Prophet\'s death, under Abu Bakr\'s caliphate. Abu Bakr insisted on honoring the Prophet\'s final military order, even though many argued the army was needed to defend Medina during the Ridda wars. Abu Bakr said: "I will not fold a banner that the Prophet \uFDFA unfurled."',
        didYouKnowAr: 'زحف جيش أسامة أخيرًا بعد وفاة النبيّ في عهد أبي بكر. أصرّ أبو بكر على تنفيذ آخر أمر عسكري للنبيّ رغم جدل كثيرين أنّ الجيش مطلوب للدفاع عن المدينة خلال حروب الردّة. قال أبو بكر: "لا أحلّ رايةً عقدها رسول الله \uFDFA."'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'merit_over_lineage', x: 0.3, y: 0.32, icon: '⚖️', label: 'The Principle', labelAr: 'المبدأ',
        fragment: 'Usama\'s appointment was the Prophet\'s final statement on leadership: it is based on merit, not age, not lineage, not race. A twenty-year-old son of a freed slave was chosen over veterans of every major battle. The message was clear: in Islam, the best person for the job gets the job, regardless of social expectations. It was the same principle that put Bilal on the Ka\'bah and Zayd at the head of an army at Mu\'tah.',
        fragmentAr: 'كان تعيين أسامة بيان النبيّ \uFDFA الأخير عن القيادة: تقوم على الجدارة لا السنّ ولا النسب ولا العرق. شابّ في العشرين ابن عبدٍ محرّر اختير على قدامى كلّ معركة كبرى. الرسالة واضحة: في الإسلام الأكفأ للمنصب يأخذه بغضّ النظر عن التوقّعات الاجتماعية. المبدأ ذاته الذي أصعد بلالًا على الكعبة وزيدًا على رأس جيش مؤتة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_army_waits', x: 0.7, y: 0.32, icon: '⏳', label: 'Camped at Jurf', labelAr: 'معسكر في الجُرف',
        fragment: 'Usama\'s army camped at al-Jurf, on the outskirts of Medina, waiting for the Prophet\'s health to improve. The army was ready. The orders were clear. But the Prophet \uFDFA was getting worse, not better. The flag that the Prophet \uFDFA had tied with his own hands hung waiting at the camp. It would not be carried into battle until after the Prophet\'s death, but his final military order would be honored to the letter.',
        fragmentAr: 'عسكر جيش أسامة في الجُرف على أطراف المدينة ينتظر تحسّن صحّة النبيّ. الجيش جاهز والأوامر واضحة. لكنّ صحّة النبيّ \uFDFA تسوء لا تتحسّن. الراية التي عقدها النبيّ \uFDFA بيده معلّقة في المعسكر تنتظر. لن تُحمل إلى المعركة حتّى بعد وفاة النبيّ لكنّ أمره العسكري الأخير سيُنفَّذ حرفيًّا.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'The wisdom of the expedition became clear after the Prophet\'s death: when Arab tribes saw that even during a leadership transition, the Muslim state could project military force to the Byzantine border, many abandoned their plans to revolt. The expedition had a deterrent effect that Abu Bakr fully understood.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'اتّضحت حكمة الحملة بعد وفاة النبيّ: حين رأت القبائل العربية أنّه حتّى خلال انتقال السلطة يستطيع الدولة الإسلامية إسقاط قوّة عسكرية على الحدود البيزنطية تخلّى كثيرون عن خطط التمرّد. كان للحملة أثر رادع فهمه أبو بكر تمامًا.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.78), Offset(0.4, 0.65), Offset(0.3, 0.55), Offset(0.5, 0.55), Offset(0.7, 0.55), Offset(0.6, 0.42), Offset(0.3, 0.32), Offset(0.5, 0.32), Offset(0.7, 0.32)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 145: The Illness Begins (632 CE) — Pattern C ─
  'j_m4_145': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_fever', x: 0.5, y: 0.6, icon: '🤒', label: 'An Unrelenting Heat', labelAr: 'حرارة لا تهدأ',
        fragment: 'The illness began in late Safar, 11 AH. The Prophet \uFDFA developed a severe fever and debilitating headache. The fever was so intense that when Ibn Mas\'ud رضي الله عنه placed his hand on the Prophet\'s forehead, he said: "You have a very severe fever." The Prophet \uFDFA replied: "I suffer twice as much as any of you." When asked why, he said: "Because I will have twice the reward." Even in agony, he turned suffering into a lesson.',
        fragmentAr: 'بدأ المرض في أواخر صفر من السنة الحادية عشرة. أصيب النبيّ \uFDFA بحمّى شديدة وصداعٍ موهن. كانت الحمّى شديدة حتّى إنّ ابن مسعود رضي الله عنه حين وضع يده على جبينه قال: "إنّك لتوعك وعكًا شديدًا." فقال النبيّ \uFDFA: "أجل إنّي أوعك كما يوعك رجلان منكم." فسُئل لماذا فقال: "لأنّ لي أجرين." حتّى في الألم حوّل المعاناة إلى درس.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #5647; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #5647; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'moving_to_aisha_s_room', x: 0.22, y: 0.42, icon: '🏠', label: 'Where He Would Stay', labelAr: 'حيث سيبقى',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA had multiple wives and normally rotated among their rooms. As the illness progressed, he kept asking: "Where am I tomorrow? Where am I tomorrow?" His wives understood: he wanted to be in Aisha\'s room. They gave their permission, and he was moved to Aisha\'s chamber, where he would remain for the rest of his illness. Aisha رضي الله عنها nursed him, cradled his head, and stayed with him until the end.',
        fragmentAr: 'كان للنبيّ \uFDFA زوجات عدّة ويتنقّل بين غرفهنّ عادةً. مع تقدّم المرض ظلّ يسأل: "أين أنا غدًا؟ أين أنا غدًا؟" فهمت زوجاته: يريد أن يكون في غرفة عائشة. فأذنّ له ونُقل إلى حجرة عائشة حيث سيبقى بقيّة مرضه. تولّت عائشة رضي الله عنها رعايته وأسندت رأسه وبقيت معه حتّى النهاية.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4442; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4442; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'Aisha رضي الله عنها used to recite the mu\'awwidhat (protective surahs: al-Falaq and al-Nas) and blow gently over the Prophet \uFDFA, then wipe his body with his own hand, because she believed his hand had more blessing than hers. This intimate detail, narrated in Sahih al-Bukhari, reveals the tenderness of their relationship in his final days.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'كانت عائشة رضي الله عنها تقرأ المعوّذات (سورتي الفلق والناس) وتنفث على النبيّ \uFDFA ثمّ تمسح جسده بيده هو لأنّها تؤمن أنّ يده أكثر بركة من يدها. هذا التفصيل الحميم المروي في صحيح البخاري يكشف حنان علاقتهما في أيامه الأخيرة.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'still_leading_prayer', x: 0.5, y: 0.25, icon: '🕌', label: 'Walking to the Mosque', labelAr: 'المشي إلى المسجد',
        fragment: 'Despite the severity of his illness, the Prophet \uFDFA continued to lead prayers in the mosque whenever he could. He would be helped by Ali and Abbas, his feet dragging on the ground, to reach the mosque. He insisted on leading his community in prayer for as long as his body could bear it. When he finally could not stand, he ordered Abu Bakr to lead the prayer in his place — a decision that would carry enormous weight in the days to come.',
        fragmentAr: 'رغم شدّة مرضه واصل النبيّ \uFDFA إمامة الصلاة في المسجد كلّما استطاع. كان يُحمل بين عليّ والعباس وقدماه تخطّان الأرض ليصل المسجد. أصرّ على إمامة أمّته في الصلاة ما دام جسده يحتمل. فحين لم يعد يستطيع الوقوف أمر أبا بكر بإمامة الصلاة بدلًا منه — قرارٌ سيحمل ثقلًا هائلًا في الأيام القادمة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #713; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #713; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_community_watches', x: 0.78, y: 0.42, icon: '😰', label: 'Fear in Medina', labelAr: 'خوف في المدينة',
        fragment: 'Medina was gripped by fear. The man who had been their leader for ten years, their prophet for twenty-three, was visibly dying. Companions gathered outside his door. Some wept. Some prayed. Some simply stood in silence, unable to imagine a world without him. The children of the Ansar, who had grown up with him in their streets, pressed close to his chamber. Everyone knew. No one was ready.',
        fragmentAr: 'قبض الخوف على المدينة. الرجل الذي كان قائدهم عشر سنوات ونبيّهم ثلاثًا وعشرين يحتضر أمام أعينهم. تجمّع الصحابة خارج بابه. بعضهم بكى. بعضهم صلّى. بعضهم وقف في صمتٍ عاجزًا عن تخيّل عالمٍ بدونه. أطفال الأنصار الذين نشأوا وهو في شوارعهم اقتربوا من حجرته. الكلّ يعلم. لا أحد مستعدّ.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'The Prophet \uFDFA had a container of water by his bed. He would dip his hand in it and wipe his face, saying: "There is no god but Allah. Indeed, death has its agonies." He did not pretend the pain was easy. He was honest about suffering, even as he remained patient. This honesty in the face of death is one of the most human moments in the entire Seerah.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'كان بجانب فراش النبيّ \uFDFA إناء ماء. يغمس يده فيه ويمسح وجهه قائلًا: "لا إله إلّا الله، إنّ للموت لسكرات." لم يتظاهر بأنّ الألم هيّن. كان صادقًا مع المعاناة حتّى وهو صابر. هذا الصدق في مواجهة الموت من أكثر لحظات السيرة إنسانية.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.78), Offset(0.5, 0.72), Offset(0.5, 0.6), Offset(0.36, 0.52), Offset(0.22, 0.42), Offset(0.28, 0.32), Offset(0.5, 0.25), Offset(0.72, 0.32), Offset(0.78, 0.42)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 146: Visiting Graves of Uhud (632 CE) — Pattern A ─
  'j_m4_146': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_journey_to_uhud', x: 0.5, y: 0.62, icon: '🏔️', label: 'One Last Visit', labelAr: 'زيارة أخيرة',
        fragment: 'Despite his illness, the Prophet \uFDFA traveled to the graves of the Uhud martyrs. He prayed for them as he had many times before, but this time there was a finality to his words. He said: "I am a witness over you. By Allah, I am looking at my Hawd (Pool in Paradise) right now." He prayed for the dead as though preparing to join them. The companions who accompanied him felt the weight of the visit. This was not a routine prayer. It was a goodbye.',
        fragmentAr: 'رغم مرضه سافر النبيّ \uFDFA إلى قبور شهداء أُحد. دعا لهم كما فعل مرّات كثيرة لكن هذه المرّة كان في كلماته نهائية. قال: "إنّي فرطكم وإنّي شهيدٌ عليكم. والله إنّي لأنظر إلى حوضي الآن." دعا للموتى كمن يستعدّ للّحاق بهم. شعر الصحابة المرافقون بثقل الزيارة. لم تكن صلاة روتينية بل كانت وداعًا.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #1344; Sahih Muslim #974',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #1344; Sahih Muslim #974'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'hamza_s_grave', x: 0.25, y: 0.44, icon: '💔', label: 'The Lion of Allah', labelAr: 'أسد الله',
        fragment: 'Among the graves was Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib رضي الله عنه — the Prophet\'s uncle, the Lion of Allah, killed at Uhud and mutilated by Hind. Eight years had passed since that day. The Prophet \uFDFA stood at his uncle\'s grave and prayed. Hamza had been one of the first to support him, one of the fiercest in his defense, and one of the most painful losses. Now the nephew was preparing to follow the uncle.',
        fragmentAr: 'بين القبور كان حمزة بن عبد المطلب رضي الله عنه عمّ النبيّ أسد الله الذي قُتل في أُحد ومثّلت به هند. مرّت ثماني سنوات على ذلك اليوم. وقف النبيّ \uFDFA عند قبر عمّه ودعا. كان حمزة من أوّل من ناصره ومن أشدّ المدافعين عنه ومن أكثر الخسائر إيلامًا. الآن ابن الأخ يستعدّ للّحاق بالعمّ.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'The Prophet \uFDFA said at Uhud: "Peace be upon you, O martyrs. You are the forerunners, and we will follow." The word "forerunners" (faratkum) implies that the dead have arrived first and the living are still on the way. It reframes death not as separation but as a delayed reunion.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'قال النبيّ \uFDFA في أُحد: "السلام عليكم يا أهل القبور، أنتم لنا فرط ونحن لكم تبع." كلمة "فرط" تعني أنّ الموتى وصلوا أوّلًا والأحياء لا يزالون في الطريق. تعيد صياغة الموت لا كافتراقٍ بل لقاءٍ مؤجّل.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'prayer_for_the_living_through_', x: 0.72, y: 0.42, icon: '🤲', label: 'A Du\'a for Both Worlds', labelAr: 'دعاء للعالَمَين',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA prayed for the martyrs of Uhud and then turned his prayer toward the living: "O Allah, do not deprive them of their reward, do not lead them astray after them, and forgive us and them." The prayer bridged two worlds — honoring the dead who had given everything, and protecting the living who would carry on without the Prophet. It was a prayer for continuity.',
        fragmentAr: 'دعا النبيّ \uFDFA لشهداء أُحد ثمّ حوّل دعاءه نحو الأحياء: "اللهمّ لا تحرمهم أجرهم ولا تفتنهم بعدهم واغفر لنا ولهم." جسّر الدعاء بين عالمين: تكريم الموتى الذين بذلوا كلّ شيء وحماية الأحياء الذين سيُكملون بدون النبيّ. كان دعاء استمرارية.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih Muslim #974',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih Muslim #974'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_return', x: 0.5, y: 0.28, icon: '🏠', label: 'Back to Medina', labelAr: 'العودة إلى المدينة',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA returned to Medina from Uhud knowing he would not visit again. He had said his farewell to Hamza, to Mus\'ab, to the seventy who fell defending him. Medina\'s skyline appeared before him — the mosque, the palm trees, the homes of the Ansar. This city that had been his refuge for ten years would soon carry his body. But not yet. There were still a few days left. A few more prayers. A few more words.',
        fragmentAr: 'عاد النبيّ \uFDFA إلى المدينة من أُحد عالمًا أنّه لن يزور مجدّدًا. ودّع حمزة ومصعبًا والسبعين الذين سقطوا دفاعًا عنه. ظهر أفق المدينة أمامه: المسجد والنخيل وبيوت الأنصار. هذه المدينة التي كانت ملاذه عشر سنوات ستحمل جثمانه قريبًا. لكن ليس بعد. لا تزال هناك أيام قليلة. صلوات أخرى. كلمات أخيرة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'Mount Uhud itself held special meaning for the Prophet \uFDFA. He once said: "Uhud is a mountain that loves us and we love it." The mountain that had witnessed the Muslims\' most painful defeat was also the mountain the Prophet \uFDFA loved most. In his final visit, that love was palpable.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'جبل أُحد نفسه يحمل معنىً خاصًّا للنبيّ \uFDFA. قال مرّة: "أُحد جبلٌ يحبّنا ونحبّه." الجبل الذي شهد أشدّ هزائم المسلمين إيلامًا كان أيضًا الجبل الذي أحبّه النبيّ \uFDFA أكثر. في زيارته الأخيرة كان هذا الحبّ ملموسًا.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.8), Offset(0.51, 0.72), Offset(0.5, 0.62), Offset(0.38, 0.54), Offset(0.25, 0.44), Offset(0.45, 0.42), Offset(0.72, 0.42), Offset(0.63, 0.36), Offset(0.5, 0.28)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 147: Abu Bakr Leads the Prayer (632 CE) — Pattern B ─
  'j_m4_147': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_order', x: 0.22, y: 0.52, icon: '📢', label: '"Tell Abu Bakr"', labelAr: '"مُروا أبا بكر"',
        fragment: 'When the Prophet \uFDFA could no longer stand to lead prayer, he said: "Tell Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer." Aisha رضي الله عنها tried to redirect, saying Abu Bakr was a soft-hearted man who would weep and the people would not hear him. She suggested Umar instead. The Prophet \uFDFA repeated firmly: "Tell Abu Bakr to lead the prayer." He said it three times. The matter was settled.',
        fragmentAr: 'حين لم يعد النبيّ \uFDFA يستطيع الوقوف للإمامة قال: "مُروا أبا بكر فليصلِّ بالناس." حاولت عائشة رضي الله عنها توجيه الأمر قائلة إنّ أبا بكر رجلٌ رقيق يبكي ولن يسمعه الناس واقترحت عمر. فكرّر النبيّ \uFDFA بحزم: "مُروا أبا بكر فليصلِّ بالناس." قالها ثلاث مرّات. حُسم الأمر.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #713; Sahih Muslim #418',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #713; Sahih Muslim #418'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'abu_bakr_weeps', x: 0.42, y: 0.46, icon: '😢', label: 'The Weight of Standing There', labelAr: 'ثقل الوقوف هناك',
        fragment: 'Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه stepped into the Prophet\'s place at the front of the mosque. He wept. Aisha had been right — he was a man of deep emotion. But his tears did not make him weak. They made him human. He led the prayer through his tears, his voice breaking, the entire congregation weeping with him. Everyone understood what it meant: the Prophet \uFDFA could no longer stand before them. The end was visible.',
        fragmentAr: 'وقف أبو بكر رضي الله عنه في مكان النبيّ \uFDFA أمام المسجد. فبكى. كانت عائشة محقّة — رجل عميق المشاعر. لكنّ دموعه لم تجعله ضعيفًا بل جعلته إنسانًا. أمّ الصلاة عبر دموعه وصوته يتقطّع والجماعة كلّها تبكي معه. الجميع فهم ماذا يعني: النبيّ \uFDFA لم يعد يستطيع الوقوف أمامهم. النهاية مرئية.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #713; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #713; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'Abu Bakr led seventeen prayers in the Prophet\'s place during the final illness. The fact that the Prophet \uFDFA chose Abu Bakr — not Umar, not Ali, not anyone else — was later cited by many scholars as an indication of who the Prophet \uFDFA wanted to succeed him. "Allah and the believers refuse anyone but Abu Bakr," Aisha later said.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'أمّ أبو بكر سبع عشرة صلاة مكان النبيّ خلال المرض الأخير. اختيار النبيّ \uFDFA لأبي بكر لا عمر ولا عليّ ولا غيرهم استشهد به كثيرٌ من العلماء لاحقًا كدلالة على من أراد النبيّ \uFDFA خلفًا له. قالت عائشة لاحقًا: "أبى الله والمؤمنون إلّا أبا بكر."'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_prophet_appears', x: 0.62, y: 0.4, icon: '✨', label: 'One Moment of Joy', labelAr: 'لحظة فرح واحدة',
        fragment: 'During one of Abu Bakr\'s prayers, the curtain of Aisha\'s chamber lifted and the Prophet \uFDFA appeared, looking at his ummah praying. His face was bright. Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه said: "I never saw his face more beautiful than at that moment — he was smiling." The congregation was overjoyed, thinking he had recovered. Abu Bakr stepped back to let the Prophet \uFDFA lead. The Prophet \uFDFA gestured: continue. It was his last look at his community in prayer.',
        fragmentAr: 'أثناء إحدى صلوات أبي بكر رُفع ستار حجرة عائشة وظهر النبيّ \uFDFA ينظر إلى أمّته تصلّي. كان وجهه مشرقًا. قال أنس بن مالك رضي الله عنه: "ما رأيت وجهه أحسن منه في تلك اللحظة كان يتبسّم." فرحت الجماعة ظنًّا أنّه تعافى. تراجع أبو بكر ليدع النبيّ \uFDFA يؤمّ. فأشار النبيّ \uFDFA: أكمل. كانت آخر نظرة إلى أمّته في الصلاة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #681; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #681; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_significance', x: 0.8, y: 0.34, icon: '🕌', label: 'The Mosque After Muhammad', labelAr: 'المسجد بعد محمد',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA had built this mosque with his own hands. He had led every prayer in it for ten years. Now another man stood in his place. The transition — prophet to companion, revelation to memory, presence to legacy — was happening one prayer at a time. The mosque would continue after him. The prayers would continue. The community would continue. That was the point. The building outlasts the builder. The ummah outlasts the prophet. That was always the design.',
        fragmentAr: 'بنى النبيّ \uFDFA هذا المسجد بيديه. أمّ كلّ صلاة فيه عشر سنوات. الآن يقف رجلٌ آخر مكانه. الانتقال من النبوّة إلى الصحبة ومن الوحي إلى الذاكرة ومن الحضور إلى الإرث يحدث صلاةً بصلاة. المسجد سيستمرّ بعده. الصلوات ستستمرّ. المجتمع سيستمرّ. تلك هي النقطة. البناء يبقى بعد البنّاء. الأمّة تبقى بعد النبيّ. هكذا صُمّم دائمًا.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'The Prophet \uFDFA reportedly said during this period: "I wanted to forbid something but then I thought: Ibrahim was the Khalil of Allah, Musa spoke directly to Allah, and I am the beloved of Allah." These words, said during his final days, show a man at peace with his life\'s work and his place among the prophets.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'رُوي أنّ النبيّ \uFDFA قال في هذه الفترة: "لقد هممت أن أنهى عن شيء ثمّ فكرت: إبراهيم خليل الله وموسى كليم الله وأنا حبيب الله." هذه الكلمات التي قيلت في أيامه الأخيرة تُظهر رجلًا في سلامٍ مع عمل حياته ومكانته بين الأنبياء.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.12, 0.58), Offset(0.17, 0.55), Offset(0.22, 0.52), Offset(0.32, 0.49), Offset(0.42, 0.46), Offset(0.52, 0.43), Offset(0.62, 0.4), Offset(0.72, 0.37), Offset(0.8, 0.34)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 148: Final Appearance in the Mosque (632 CE) — Pattern D ─
  'j_m4_148': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_curtain_lifts', x: 0.5, y: 0.5, icon: '✨', label: 'The Last Smile', labelAr: 'الابتسامة الأخيرة',
        fragment: 'On the morning of Monday, 12 Rabi al-Awwal, during Fajr, the Prophet \uFDFA lifted the curtain of Aisha\'s chamber, which adjoined the mosque. Anas رضي الله عنه said: "He lifted the curtain and looked at us while we were praying behind Abu Bakr. His face was like a page of the Quran — I had never seen it more beautiful. He smiled." The companions were overwhelmed with joy, thinking recovery had come.',
        fragmentAr: 'صبيحة الاثنين الثاني عشر من ربيع الأوّل أثناء صلاة الفجر رفع النبيّ \uFDFA ستار حجرة عائشة الملاصقة للمسجد. قال أنس رضي الله عنه: "كشف الستر ونظر إلينا وهو قائم ونحن صفوف في الصلاة خلف أبي بكر. كان وجهه كأنّه ورقة مصحف ما رأيته أحسن منه قط. فتبسّم." غمر الفرح الصحابة ظنًّا أنّ الشفاء جاء.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #681',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #681'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'abu_bakr_steps_back', x: 0.25, y: 0.4, icon: '🚶', label: 'A Moment of Hope', labelAr: 'لحظة أمل',
        fragment: 'Abu Bakr, seeing the Prophet \uFDFA, stepped back to let him lead. The Prophet \uFDFA gestured with his hand: stay where you are. He signaled to Abu Bakr to continue leading the prayer and then dropped the curtain. The moment of joy — seeing his face, his smile, his presence — lasted only seconds. But it was enough. It was the Prophet\'s \uFDFA final visual farewell to his ummah. His last look at the community he had built from nothing.',
        fragmentAr: 'أبو بكر حين رأى النبيّ \uFDFA تراجع ليدعه يؤمّ. فأشار النبيّ \uFDFA بيده: ابقَ مكانك. أشار لأبي بكر أن يُكمل الصلاة ثمّ أسدل الستار. لحظة الفرح — رؤية وجهه وابتسامته وحضوره — دامت ثوانٍ فقط. لكنّها كافية. كانت وداع النبيّ \uFDFA البصري الأخير لأمّته. آخر نظرة إلى المجتمع الذي بناه من لا شيء.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #681',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #681',
        didYouKnow: 'This appearance was just hours before the Prophet\'s death. The companions who saw him smile thought the worst was over. Many returned to their normal activities, reassured. Abu Bakr even rode to his home in al-Sunh, outside Medina. The joy of that morning made the shock of that afternoon even more devastating.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'كان هذا الظهور قبل ساعاتٍ من وفاة النبيّ. الصحابة الذين رأوه يبتسم ظنّوا أنّ الأسوأ مرّ. عاد كثيرون إلى شؤونهم مطمئنّين. حتّى أبو بكر ركب إلى بيته في السُّنح خارج المدينة. فرح ذلك الصباح جعل صدمة ذلك الظهيرة أشدّ تدميرًا.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_hours_between', x: 0.75, y: 0.4, icon: '⏳', label: 'The Last Morning', labelAr: 'الصباح الأخير',
        fragment: 'After dropping the curtain, the Prophet \uFDFA returned to his bed. Aisha رضي الله عنها later described those final hours. He laid his head in her lap. He used a siwak to clean his teeth — she softened it for him first. He dipped his hand in water and wiped his face. His lips moved. Aisha leaned close. He was whispering: "With the highest companions... with the highest companions..." He had made his choice. He chose the meeting with his Lord.',
        fragmentAr: 'بعد إسدال الستار عاد النبيّ \uFDFA إلى فراشه. وصفت عائشة رضي الله عنها تلك الساعات الأخيرة. وضع رأسه في حجرها. استاك بسواك ليّنته له أوّلًا. غمس يده في الماء ومسح وجهه. شفتاه تتحرّكان. اقتربت عائشة. كان يهمس: "مع الرفيق الأعلى... مع الرفيق الأعلى..." اتّخذ قراره. اختار لقاء ربّه.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4449-4451; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4449-4451; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_passage', x: 0.5, y: 0.28, icon: '🕊️', label: '"With the Highest Companions"', labelAr: '"مع الرفيق الأعلى"',
        fragment: 'Aisha رضي الله عنها narrated: "His hand grew heavy in mine. I looked at his face. His eyes were fixed upward. He said: \'Rather the highest companions in Paradise.\' I knew then that he was not choosing us." His head was in her lap. His last words were about Paradise. His last breath was in her arms. The Prophet Muhammad \uFDFA, the final Messenger of Allah, passed away on Monday, 12 Rabi al-Awwal, 11 AH. He was sixty-three years old.',
        fragmentAr: 'روت عائشة رضي الله عنها: "ثقلت يده في يدي. نظرتُ إلى وجهه. شخصت عيناه نحو الأعلى. فقال: \'بل الرفيق الأعلى من الجنة.\' فعلمت أنّه لم يخترنا." رأسه في حجرها. كلماته الأخيرة عن الجنة. نَفَسه الأخير بين ذراعيها. النبيّ محمد \uFDFA، آخر رسل الله، انتقل إلى الرفيق الأعلى يوم الاثنين الثاني عشر من ربيع الأوّل سنة 11 للهجرة. عمره ثلاث وستون سنة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4449-4451; Sahih Muslim #2444',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4449-4451; Sahih Muslim #2444',
        didYouKnow: 'The siwak that the Prophet \uFDFA used in his final moments was given to Aisha by her brother Abdur Rahman رضي الله عنه, who entered the room holding it. The Prophet \uFDFA looked at it, and Aisha understood he wanted it. She chewed it to soften it and gave it to him. It was the last act of care she performed for him.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'السواك الذي استخدمه النبيّ \uFDFA في لحظاته الأخيرة أعطاه لعائشة أخوها عبد الرحمن رضي الله عنه الذي دخل الغرفة حاملًا إيّاه. نظر إليه النبيّ \uFDFA وفهمت عائشة أنّه يريده. فمضغته لتليّنه وأعطته إيّاه. كان آخر عمل عناية قدّمته له.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.78), Offset(0.5, 0.62), Offset(0.5, 0.5), Offset(0.36, 0.45), Offset(0.25, 0.4), Offset(0.5, 0.42), Offset(0.75, 0.4), Offset(0.62, 0.34), Offset(0.5, 0.28)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 149: The Final Days (632 CE) — Pattern F ─
  'j_m4_149': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_prophet_s_final_concerns', x: 0.3, y: 0.55, icon: '💭', label: 'Three Things on His Mind', labelAr: 'ثلاثة أشياء في باله',
        fragment: 'In his final days, the Prophet \uFDFA spoke of three things repeatedly: prayer, the treatment of slaves, and Usama\'s army. "Prayer, prayer! And fear Allah regarding those your right hand possesses." He kept asking: "Has Usama\'s army departed?" He wanted the community\'s worship intact, its moral obligation to the vulnerable upheld, and its strategic commitments honored. These three concerns — worship, justice, and defense — were the pillars he wanted to leave standing.',
        fragmentAr: 'في أيامه الأخيرة تحدّث النبيّ \uFDFA عن ثلاثة أشياء مرارًا: الصلاة ومعاملة العبيد وجيش أسامة. "الصلاة الصلاة! وما ملكت أيمانكم." ظلّ يسأل: "أنفذوا جيش أسامة؟" أراد عبادة المجتمع سليمة والتزامه الأخلاقي تجاه الضعفاء محفوظًا وتعهّداته الاستراتيجية منفَّذة. هذه الاهتمامات الثلاثة — العبادة والعدالة والدفاع — الأركان التي أراد تركها قائمة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #2916; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #2916; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_seven_dinars', x: 0.7, y: 0.55, icon: '💰', label: 'Nothing Left Behind', labelAr: 'لا شيء وراءه',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA asked Aisha: "How much money do we have?" She said: "Seven dinars." He said: "Give them away in charity." Later she checked — they were still there. He asked again. She gave them away. He said: "Now I can meet Allah." The man who had conquered Arabia, received delegations from kings, and commanded thirty thousand soldiers at Tabuk died with nothing. Not from poverty, but from choice.',
        fragmentAr: 'سأل النبيّ \uFDFA عائشة: "كم عندنا من المال؟" فقالت: "سبعة دنانير." فقال: "تصدّقي بها." تفقّدتها لاحقًا فلا تزال موجودة. سألها مجدّدًا. فتصدّقت بها. فقال: "الآن أستطيع لقاء الله." الرجل الذي فتح الجزيرة واستقبل وفود الملوك وقاد ثلاثين ألفًا في تبوك مات بلا شيء. لا من فقرٍ بل من اختيار.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Musnad Ahmad; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Musnad Ahmad; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'When the Prophet \uFDFA died, his armor was pawned with a Jewish merchant for thirty measures of barley. The leader of Arabia had borrowed money for food. Aisha رضي الله عنها later said: "He died and there was nothing on my shelf that a living creature could eat except a handful of barley."',
        didYouKnowAr: 'حين توفّي النبيّ \uFDFA كان درعه مرهونًا عند تاجر يهودي بثلاثين صاعًا من شعير. زعيم الجزيرة اقترض ثمن طعام. قالت عائشة رضي الله عنها لاحقًا: "مات وما على رفّي شيء يأكله ذو كبدٍ إلّا شطر شعير."'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'fatimah_s_visit', x: 0.3, y: 0.32, icon: '👩', label: 'A Father and Daughter', labelAr: 'أبٌ وابنة',
        fragment: 'Fatimah رضي الله عنها visited her father during his final days. She looked at his suffering and said: "O my father, what distress!" The Prophet \uFDFA replied: "Your father will have no more distress after today." He then whispered two secrets to her — the first made her cry (he was dying), the second made her laugh (she would be the first of his family to join him). Fatimah died approximately six months later.',
        fragmentAr: 'زارت فاطمة رضي الله عنها أباها في أيامه الأخيرة. نظرت إلى معاناته وقالت: "وا كرب أباه!" فقال النبيّ \uFDFA: "ليس على أبيك كرب بعد اليوم." ثمّ أسرّ لها سرّين: الأوّل أبكاها (أنّه يموت) والثاني أضحكها (أنّها أوّل أهله لحاقًا به). توفّيت فاطمة بعد نحو ستة أشهر.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4462; Sahih Muslim #2450',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4462; Sahih Muslim #2450'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_last_night', x: 0.7, y: 0.32, icon: '🌙', label: 'Between This World and the Next', labelAr: 'بين هذا العالم والآخر',
        fragment: 'The Prophet\'s last night was spent in Aisha\'s chamber. He alternated between consciousness and unconsciousness. When awake, he prayed. When the pain surged, he dipped his hand in water and wiped his face, saying: "There is no god but Allah. Death has its agonies." He recited the mu\'awwidhat. He asked about the prayer. He asked about Usama\'s army. Between this world and the next, his concerns were for his ummah, not himself.',
        fragmentAr: 'أمضى النبيّ \uFDFA ليلته الأخيرة في حجرة عائشة. تراوح بين الوعي والإغماء. حين يفيق يصلّي. حين يشتدّ الألم يغمس يده في الماء ويمسح وجهه قائلًا: "لا إله إلّا الله، إنّ للموت لسكرات." قرأ المعوّذات. سأل عن الصلاة. سأل عن جيش أسامة. بين هذا العالم والآخر كانت همومه لأمّته لا لنفسه.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4449; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4449; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'The Prophet \uFDFA had declined to leave a written will. When some companions suggested he write one, Umar said: "The Prophet\'s illness has intensified. We have the Book of Allah, and that is sufficient." The Prophet \uFDFA had already said in the Farewell Sermon: "I leave you the Book of Allah." His legacy was not a document. It was a community carrying a Book.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'رفض النبيّ \uFDFA ترك وصية مكتوبة. حين اقترح بعض الصحابة أن يكتب واحدة قال عمر: "اشتدّ بالنبيّ الوجع، عندنا كتاب الله حسبنا." كان النبيّ \uFDFA قد قال في خطبة الوداع: "تركت فيكم كتاب الله." إرثه لم يكن وثيقة بل أمّة تحمل كتابًا.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.78), Offset(0.4, 0.65), Offset(0.3, 0.55), Offset(0.5, 0.55), Offset(0.7, 0.55), Offset(0.6, 0.42), Offset(0.3, 0.32), Offset(0.5, 0.32), Offset(0.7, 0.32)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 150: The Departure (632 CE) — Pattern C ─
  'j_m4_150': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_moment', x: 0.5, y: 0.6, icon: '🕊️', label: 'He Chose the Highest', labelAr: 'اختار الرفيق الأعلى',
        fragment: 'In the late morning of Monday, 12 Rabi al-Awwal, 11 AH, the Prophet Muhammad \uFDFA, the final Messenger of Allah, departed this world. His head was in Aisha\'s lap. His last words were: "Rather the highest companions in Paradise." His hand fell. His eyes remained fixed upward. Aisha رضي الله عنها later said: "He died between my chest and my chin, and I did not wrong anyone by his death — for it was during my turn."',
        fragmentAr: 'في ضحى يوم الاثنين الثاني عشر من ربيع الأوّل سنة 11 للهجرة انتقل النبيّ محمد \uFDFA آخر رسل الله إلى الرفيق الأعلى. رأسه في حجر عائشة. كلماته الأخيرة: "بل الرفيق الأعلى من الجنة." سقطت يده. بقيت عيناه مثبّتتين نحو الأعلى. قالت عائشة رضي الله عنها لاحقًا: "قُبض بين سحري ونحري ولم أظلم أحدًا بموته فإنّه كان في يومي."',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4449-4451; Sahih Muslim #2444',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #4449-4451; Sahih Muslim #2444'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_news_spreads', x: 0.22, y: 0.42, icon: '😢', label: 'Medina Breaks', labelAr: 'المدينة تنكسر',
        fragment: 'The news spread through Medina like a shockwave. Aisha\'s grief was heard through the walls. The companions outside the chamber stumbled. Some collapsed. Some refused to believe it. The city that had been his home for ten years convulsed with grief. The market fell silent. The streets emptied. Children cried without understanding why. Medina had lost not just a leader or a prophet — it had lost the man who had made it Medina.',
        fragmentAr: 'انتشر الخبر في المدينة كموجة صدمة. سُمع نحيب عائشة عبر الجدران. تعثّر الصحابة خارج الحجرة. بعضهم انهار. بعضهم رفض التصديق. المدينة التي كانت بيته عشر سنوات اهتزّت بالحزن. صمت السوق. خلت الشوارع. بكى الأطفال دون أن يفهموا لماذا. المدينة لم تفقد مجرّد قائد أو نبيّ بل الرجل الذي جعلها المدينة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه later said: "The day the Prophet \uFDFA entered Medina, everything was bright. The day he died, everything became dark. We had not yet dusted the dirt from our hands after burying him when we felt our hearts had changed." The loss was not just emotional. It was existential. The world had been reorganized around one man, and now he was gone.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'قال أنس بن مالك رضي الله عنه لاحقًا: "يوم دخل النبيّ \uFDFA المدينة أضاء منها كلّ شيء. ويوم مات أظلم منها كلّ شيء. وما نفضنا التراب عن أيدينا من دفنه حتّى أنكرنا قلوبنا." الخسارة لم تكن عاطفية فحسب بل وجودية. أُعيد تنظيم العالم حول رجلٍ واحد والآن رحل.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'ali_washes_the_body', x: 0.5, y: 0.25, icon: '💧', label: 'The Final Care', labelAr: 'العناية الأخيرة',
        fragment: 'Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه washed the Prophet\'s body, assisted by Abbas and his sons al-Fadl and Qutham. Ali would later say: "By my father and mother, you were pure alive and pure dead. Even in death, you were the most beautiful." They washed him in his garments, not removing them fully, as a mark of reverence. The water was drawn from the well called Ghars. The washing was done with the tenderness of family.',
        fragmentAr: 'غسّل عليّ بن أبي طالب رضي الله عنه جثمان النبيّ \uFDFA بمساعدة العباس وابنيه الفضل وقُثم. قال عليّ لاحقًا: "بأبي وأمّي طِبتَ حيًّا وطِبتَ ميّتًا. حتّى في الموت كنت أجمل الخلق." غسّلوه في ثيابه دون نزعها كاملةً إجلالًا. جُلب الماء من بئر تُسمّى غرس. كان الغسل بحنان العائلة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37; Ibn Hisham',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37; Ibn Hisham'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_world_without_him', x: 0.78, y: 0.42, icon: '🌍', label: 'The Silence After', labelAr: 'الصمت بعده',
        fragment: 'For twenty-three years, the Quran had descended. Now it stopped. For twenty-three years, a man had carried the weight of revelation between heaven and earth. Now that bridge was closed. For twenty-three years, when the community had a question, they could walk to his door and ask. Now the door was closed. The Prophet Muhammad \uFDFA had completed his mission. The religion was perfected. The message was delivered. What remained was a Book, a community, and a memory that would shape the rest of human history.',
        fragmentAr: 'ثلاثًا وعشرين سنة نزل القرآن. الآن توقّف. ثلاثًا وعشرين سنة حمل رجل ثقل الوحي بين السماء والأرض. الآن أُغلق ذلك الجسر. ثلاثًا وعشرين سنة حين يسأل المجتمع سؤالًا يمشون إلى بابه ويسألون. الآن أُغلق الباب. النبيّ محمد \uFDFA أكمل رسالته. الدين أُتِمّ. الرسالة بُلّغت. ما بقي كتابٌ وأمّة وذكرى ستشكّل بقيّة تاريخ البشرية.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'The Prophet \uFDFA died on a Monday — the same day of the week he was born, the same day he received the first revelation, and the same day he arrived in Medina during the Hijrah. The symmetry was noted by the companions. His life began and ended on the same day.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'توفّي النبيّ \uFDFA يوم الاثنين، اليوم ذاته من الأسبوع الذي وُلد فيه وتلقّى فيه أوّل وحي ووصل فيه المدينة في الهجرة. لاحظ الصحابة التماثل. حياته بدأت وانتهت في اليوم ذاته.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.78), Offset(0.5, 0.72), Offset(0.5, 0.6), Offset(0.36, 0.52), Offset(0.22, 0.42), Offset(0.28, 0.32), Offset(0.5, 0.25), Offset(0.72, 0.32), Offset(0.78, 0.42)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 151: Umar's Grief (632 CE) — Pattern A ─
  'j_m4_151': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'umar_s_denial', x: 0.5, y: 0.62, icon: '⚔️', label: '"He Has Not Died!"', labelAr: '"ما مات!"',
        fragment: 'When the news of the Prophet\'s death spread, Umar رضي الله عنه refused to accept it. He stood in the mosque courtyard, drew his sword, and declared: "Some hypocrites are claiming that the Messenger of Allah has died! He has not died! He has gone to his Lord as Musa went to his Lord for forty nights! He will return and cut off the hands and feet of those who claim he has died!" No one could calm him. No one dared approach. Umar\'s grief had turned to fury.',
        fragmentAr: 'حين انتشر خبر وفاة النبيّ رفض عمر رضي الله عنه تصديقه. وقف في ساحة المسجد واستلّ سيفه وأعلن: "إنّ رجالًا من المنافقين يزعمون أنّ رسول الله \uFDFA قد مات! إنّه لم يمت! ذهب إلى ربّه كما ذهب موسى إلى ربّه أربعين ليلة! وسيرجع فيقطع أيدي رجالٍ وأرجلهم يزعمون أنّه قد مات!" لم يستطع أحدٌ تهدئته. لم يجرؤ أحدٌ على الاقتراب. حزن عمر تحوّل إلى غضب.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3668; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3668; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_strongest_man_breaks', x: 0.25, y: 0.44, icon: '💔', label: 'Grief Beyond Words', labelAr: 'حزنٌ فوق الكلمات',
        fragment: 'Umar\'s denial was not rational — it was emotional. This was the man who had been by the Prophet\'s side for twenty years. The man who donated half his wealth for Tabuk. The man who had been offered the caliphate by implication when the Prophet \uFDFA ordered Abu Bakr to lead prayer instead. Umar could face any enemy, any battle, any threat. He could not face this. The strongest man in Medina could not bear the weight of one death.',
        fragmentAr: 'إنكار عمر لم يكن عقلانيًّا بل عاطفيًّا. هذا الرجل الذي كان إلى جانب النبيّ عشرين سنة. الذي تبرّع بنصف ماله لتبوك. الذي عُرضت عليه الخلافة ضمنيًّا حين أمر النبيّ \uFDFA أبا بكر بإمامة الصلاة بدلًا منه. عمر يستطيع مواجهة أيّ عدوّ وأيّ معركة وأيّ تهديد. لم يستطع مواجهة هذا. أقوى رجل في المدينة لم يحتمل ثقل موتٍ واحد.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3668; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3668; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'Umar later said: "When I heard Abu Bakr recite that verse, my legs could not support me and I fell to the ground. I knew then that the Prophet \uFDFA had truly died." The man who could not be defeated in battle was defeated by a single Quranic verse. It was the most human moment of his life.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'قال عمر لاحقًا: "حين سمعت أبا بكر يتلو تلك الآية لم تحملني رجلاي فسقطت على الأرض. علمت عندها أنّ النبيّ \uFDFA قد مات حقًّا." الرجل الذي لم يُهزم في معركة هزمته آية قرآنية واحدة. كانت أكثر لحظات حياته إنسانية.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_community_paralyzed', x: 0.72, y: 0.42, icon: '😰', label: 'No One Knows What to Do', labelAr: 'لا أحد يعرف ماذا يفعل',
        fragment: 'With Umar threatening anyone who claimed the Prophet \uFDFA had died, and the rest of the companions in shock, Medina was paralyzed. Some sat weeping. Some paced in confusion. Some repeated Umar\'s words, hoping they were true. The community that had been led by one man for twenty-three years suddenly had no leader, no guidance, and no precedent. No prophet had ever died in front of his community before. They had no script for this.',
        fragmentAr: 'مع تهديد عمر لكلّ من يدّعي أنّ النبيّ \uFDFA مات وبقيّة الصحابة في صدمة أُصيبت المدينة بالشلل. بعضهم جلس يبكي. بعضهم يمشي في حيرة. بعضهم يردّد كلمات عمر آملًا أنّها صحيحة. المجتمع الذي قاده رجلٌ واحد ثلاثًا وعشرين سنة أصبح فجأة بلا قائد ولا توجيه ولا سابقة. لم يمت نبيّ أمام أمّته من قبل. ليس لديهم نصٌّ لهذا.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'waiting_for_abu_bakr', x: 0.5, y: 0.28, icon: '⏳', label: 'The One Who Could Speak', labelAr: 'الذي يستطيع الكلام',
        fragment: 'Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه was at his home in al-Sunh, outside Medina. He had left that morning reassured by the Prophet\'s smile at Fajr. When the news reached him, he rode back immediately. He entered Aisha\'s chamber, knelt beside the Prophet\'s body, kissed his forehead, and wept quietly: "How beautiful you are alive and dead." Then he stood, dried his eyes, and walked into the mosque. What he said next would save the community from collapse.',
        fragmentAr: 'كان أبو بكر رضي الله عنه في بيته بالسُّنح خارج المدينة. غادر ذلك الصباح مطمئنًّا بابتسامة النبيّ في الفجر. فحين وصله الخبر عاد فورًا. دخل حجرة عائشة وركع بجانب جثمان النبيّ وقبّل جبينه وبكى بهدوء: "طِبتَ حيًّا وميّتًا." ثمّ وقف ومسح دموعه ومشى إلى المسجد. ما سيقوله الآن سينقذ المجتمع من الانهيار.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3668; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3668; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'Abu Bakr entered Aisha\'s chamber before going to the mosque. He needed to see for himself. He uncovered the Prophet\'s face, kissed him, and said: "You are too noble for Allah to give you death twice. The death that was written for you has now passed." Only after this private moment of farewell did he go public. He grieved privately, then led publicly. That sequence defined leadership.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'دخل أبو بكر حجرة عائشة قبل المسجد. احتاج أن يرى بنفسه. كشف وجه النبيّ وقبّله وقال: "إنّك أكرم على الله من أن يجمع عليك موتتين. أمّا الموتة التي كُتبت عليك فقد متّها." فقط بعد لحظة الوداع الخاصّة هذه ذهب إلى العلن. حزن سرًّا ثمّ قاد علنًا. هذا التسلسل عرّف القيادة.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.8), Offset(0.51, 0.72), Offset(0.5, 0.62), Offset(0.38, 0.54), Offset(0.25, 0.44), Offset(0.45, 0.42), Offset(0.72, 0.42), Offset(0.63, 0.36), Offset(0.5, 0.28)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 152: Abu Bakr's Address (632 CE) — Pattern B ─
  'j_m4_152': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'sit_down_umar', x: 0.22, y: 0.52, icon: '✋', label: 'The Calm Voice', labelAr: 'الصوت الهادئ',
        fragment: 'Abu Bakr entered the mosque and saw Umar raging. He said: "Sit down, Umar." Umar did not sit. Abu Bakr ignored him and turned to the people. He said: "O people, whoever worshipped Muhammad, let him know that Muhammad has died. And whoever worshipped Allah, let him know that Allah is alive and does not die." The simplicity was devastating. In one sentence, he separated the eternal from the mortal.',
        fragmentAr: 'دخل أبو بكر المسجد ورأى عمر ثائرًا. قال: "اجلس يا عمر." لم يجلس عمر. تجاهله أبو بكر والتفت إلى الناس. قال: "أيّها الناس، من كان يعبد محمدًا فإنّ محمدًا قد مات. ومن كان يعبد الله فإنّ الله حيٌّ لا يموت." البساطة كانت مدمّرة. في جملة واحدة فصل الأبدي عن الفاني.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3668',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3668'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_verse', x: 0.42, y: 0.46, icon: '📖', label: 'The Quran Answers', labelAr: 'القرآن يجيب',
        fragment: 'Then Abu Bakr recited: "Muhammad is not but a messenger. Other messengers have passed on before him. So if he were to die or be killed, would you turn back on your heels? And whoever turns back on his heels will never harm Allah at all." (3:144). The verse had been revealed after Uhud, when a rumor of the Prophet\'s death had caused panic. Now, years later, the same verse answered the same question for the last time. The companions heard it as though hearing it for the first time.',
        fragmentAr: 'ثمّ تلا أبو بكر: ﴿وَمَا مُحَمَّدٌ إِلَّا رَسُولٌ قَدْ خَلَتْ مِنْ قَبْلِهِ الرُّسُلُ أَفَإِنْ مَاتَ أَوْ قُتِلَ انْقَلَبْتُمْ عَلَىٰ أَعْقَابِكُمْ وَمَنْ يَنْقَلِبْ عَلَىٰ عَقِبَيْهِ فَلَنْ يَضُرَّ اللَّهَ شَيْئًا﴾. الآية نزلت بعد أُحد حين سبّبت إشاعة مقتل النبيّ هلعًا. الآن بعد سنوات الآية ذاتها تجيب السؤال ذاته للمرّة الأخيرة. سمعها الصحابة كأنّهم يسمعونها لأوّل مرّة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Quran 3:144; Sahih al-Bukhari #3668',
        sourceRefAr: 'Quran 3:144; Sahih al-Bukhari #3668',
        didYouKnow: 'Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه later said: "By Allah, it was as though the people never knew this verse had been revealed until Abu Bakr recited it. Everyone was repeating it — you could not hear anyone in Medina who was not reciting this verse." A verse revealed years earlier suddenly became the most recited verse in the city.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'قال ابن عباس رضي الله عنه لاحقًا: "والله لكأنّ الناس لم يعلموا أنّ هذه الآية نزلت حتّى تلاها أبو بكر. فجعل الناس يأخذونها عنه فما تسمع بشرًا في المدينة إلّا يتلوها." آية نزلت قبل سنوات أصبحت فجأة الأكثر تلاوة في المدينة.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'umar_falls', x: 0.62, y: 0.4, icon: '😢', label: 'The Sword Drops', labelAr: 'السيف يسقط',
        fragment: 'When Umar heard Abu Bakr recite 3:144, his legs gave way. He fell to the ground. The sword dropped. The denial shattered. He later said: "When I heard Abu Bakr recite that verse, I was struck with terror. My legs could not support me and I collapsed, knowing that the Prophet \uFDFA had indeed died." The strongest man in Medina was brought to his knees not by a sword or an army, but by one verse of the Quran and one calm voice speaking it.',
        fragmentAr: 'حين سمع عمر أبا بكر يتلو الآية 144 من آل عمران خانته رجلاه. سقط على الأرض. سقط السيف. تحطّم الإنكار. قال لاحقًا: "حين سمعت أبا بكر يتلو تلك الآية صُعقت. لم تحملني رجلاي فسقطت عالمًا أنّ النبيّ \uFDFA قد مات حقًّا." أقوى رجل في المدينة أُسقط على ركبتيه لا بسيفٍ أو جيش بل بآية قرآنية واحدة وصوت هادئ يتلوها.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3668',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3668'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_community_holds', x: 0.8, y: 0.34, icon: '🏛️', label: 'Islam Continues', labelAr: 'الإسلام يستمرّ',
        fragment: 'Abu Bakr\'s address saved the Muslim community from collapse. In sixty seconds, he accomplished three things: he confirmed the death, he separated worship of Allah from love of Muhammad, and he reminded the community that the Quran had already prepared them for this moment. The community wept, but it held. The mosque still stood. The prayers still continued. The Book still existed. Muhammad \uFDFA had died. Islam had not.',
        fragmentAr: 'خطاب أبي بكر أنقذ المجتمع المسلم من الانهيار. في ستين ثانية أنجز ثلاثة أشياء: أكّد الوفاة وفصل عبادة الله عن حبّ محمد وذكّر المجتمع بأنّ القرآن أعدّهم لهذه اللحظة مسبقًا. بكى المجتمع لكنّه صمد. المسجد لا يزال قائمًا. الصلوات لا تزال مستمرّة. الكتاب لا يزال موجودًا. محمد \uFDFA مات. الإسلام لم يمت.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3668; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3668; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'Abu Bakr\'s speech is studied as one of the greatest crisis communications in history. He addressed denial (Umar\'s), provided evidence (the verse), reframed the narrative (worship Allah, not Muhammad), and gave the community a path forward — all in under a minute. No leader in history has ever stabilized a community faster.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'يُدرَس خطاب أبي بكر كواحد من أعظم خطابات الأزمات في التاريخ. عالج الإنكار (عمر) وقدّم الدليل (الآية) وأعاد صياغة السردية (اعبدوا الله لا محمدًا) ومنح المجتمع مسارًا للأمام — كلّ ذلك في أقلّ من دقيقة. لم يستقرّ قائد في التاريخ مجتمعًا أسرع من ذلك.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.12, 0.58), Offset(0.17, 0.55), Offset(0.22, 0.52), Offset(0.32, 0.49), Offset(0.42, 0.46), Offset(0.52, 0.43), Offset(0.62, 0.4), Offset(0.72, 0.37), Offset(0.8, 0.34)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 153: Funeral Prayer (632 CE) — Pattern D ─
  'j_m4_153': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'no_imam', x: 0.5, y: 0.5, icon: '🤲', label: 'Individual Prayer', labelAr: 'صلاة فردية',
        fragment: 'The funeral prayer over the Prophet \uFDFA was unique in Islamic history: there was no imam. The companions could not agree on who should lead it, and no one had the heart to stand in front of the Prophet\'s body and lead prayer. Instead, groups entered the small chamber, stood before him, and prayed individually. Each person\'s prayer was personal, direct, and unmediated. It was as though each Muslim was saying goodbye alone.',
        fragmentAr: 'صلاة الجنازة على النبيّ \uFDFA فريدة في التاريخ الإسلامي: لم يكن هناك إمام. لم يتّفق الصحابة على من يؤمّ ولم يحتمل أحدٌ الوقوف أمام جثمان النبيّ لإمامة الصلاة. بدلًا من ذلك دخلت مجموعات الحجرة الصغيرة ووقفت أمامه وصلّت فرادى. صلاة كلّ شخص كانت شخصية ومباشرة وبلا وسيط. كأنّ كلّ مسلمٍ يودّع وحده.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37; Ibn Hisham',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37; Ibn Hisham'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'wave_after_wave', x: 0.25, y: 0.4, icon: '🌊', label: 'All of Medina', labelAr: 'المدينة كلّها',
        fragment: 'The funeral prayers continued for over a day. First the men of the Muhajirun, then the Ansar, then the women, then the children. Group after group entered the chamber, prayed, wept, and left. The process took from Tuesday morning through Wednesday. Every Muslim in Medina prayed over the Prophet \uFDFA individually. The city that had welcomed him ten years ago was saying goodbye one soul at a time.',
        fragmentAr: 'استمرّت صلوات الجنازة أكثر من يوم. أوّلًا رجال المهاجرين ثمّ الأنصار ثمّ النساء ثمّ الأطفال. مجموعة تلو مجموعة دخلت الحجرة وصلّت وبكت وخرجت. استغرقت العملية من صباح الثلاثاء إلى الأربعاء. كلّ مسلم في المدينة صلّى على النبيّ \uFDFA فرديًّا. المدينة التي رحّبت به قبل عشر سنوات تودّعه روحًا بروح.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'The delay in burial was deliberate: the companions wanted every Muslim in Medina to have the chance to pray over the Prophet \uFDFA. In normal Islamic practice, burial is hastened. But this was not a normal death. The city needed time to grieve, to pray, and to accept.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'التأخير في الدفن كان مقصودًا: أراد الصحابة أن تُتاح الفرصة لكلّ مسلم في المدينة للصلاة على النبيّ \uFDFA. في الممارسة الإسلامية العادية يُعجَّل الدفن. لكنّ هذا لم يكن موتًا عاديًّا. المدينة احتاجت وقتًا للحزن والصلاة والقبول.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_last_look', x: 0.75, y: 0.4, icon: '👁️', label: 'Seeing Him One Final Time', labelAr: 'رؤيته للمرّة الأخيرة',
        fragment: 'Each group that entered saw the Prophet \uFDFA lying on his bed, wrapped in his garments, his face uncovered during the prayer. Ali had said during the washing: "You were pure alive and pure dead." Now the companions saw for themselves: even in death, his face was serene. Many would later say they had never seen him more at peace. The anguish of the illness was gone. What remained was a man who had completed his mission.',
        fragmentAr: 'كلّ مجموعة دخلت رأت النبيّ \uFDFA مسجّىً على فراشه ملفوفًا بثيابه ووجهه مكشوفًا أثناء الصلاة. قال عليّ أثناء الغسل: "طِبتَ حيًّا وميّتًا." الآن رأى الصحابة بأنفسهم: حتّى في الموت وجهه هادئ. كثيرون قالوا لاحقًا إنّهم لم يروه أكثر سلامًا. ألم المرض زال. ما بقي رجلٌ أكمل رسالته.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'where_to_bury', x: 0.5, y: 0.28, icon: '❓', label: 'The Debate', labelAr: 'النقاش',
        fragment: 'A debate arose: where should the Prophet \uFDFA be buried? Some suggested the mosque. Some suggested al-Baqi cemetery with his companions. Abu Bakr resolved it with a hadith: "I heard the Prophet \uFDFA say: \'Every prophet is buried where he dies.\'" The bed was lifted, the ground beneath was dug, and the grave was prepared in the exact spot where the Prophet \uFDFA had drawn his last breath — inside Aisha\'s chamber.',
        fragmentAr: 'نشأ نقاش: أين يُدفن النبيّ \uFDFA؟ اقترح بعضهم المسجد. واقترح آخرون مقبرة البقيع مع صحابته. حسمه أبو بكر بحديث: "سمعت النبيّ \uFDFA يقول: \'كلّ نبيّ يُدفن حيث يموت.\'" رُفع الفراش وحُفرت الأرض تحته وأُعدّ القبر في المكان ذاته الذي لفظ فيه النبيّ \uFDFA أنفاسه الأخيرة — داخل حجرة عائشة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sunan al-Tirmidhi #1018; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sunan al-Tirmidhi #1018; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'The grave was dug by Abu Talhah al-Ansari رضي الله عنه, who dug graves in the Medinan style (lahd — a niche dug into the side of the grave). The Prophet \uFDFA was placed in the lahd, and unbaked bricks were used to seal it. No coffin. No elaborate tomb. The most beloved man in Islamic history was buried in the simplest way possible.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'حفر القبر أبو طلحة الأنصاري رضي الله عنه بطريقة المدينة (اللحد — فجوة في جانب القبر). وُضع النبيّ \uFDFA في اللحد وأُغلق بلَبِنٍ غير مشوي. لا تابوت ولا ضريح فاخر. أحبّ إنسان في التاريخ الإسلامي دُفن بأبسط طريقة ممكنة.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.78), Offset(0.5, 0.62), Offset(0.5, 0.5), Offset(0.36, 0.45), Offset(0.25, 0.4), Offset(0.5, 0.42), Offset(0.75, 0.4), Offset(0.62, 0.34), Offset(0.5, 0.28)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 154: The Burial (632 CE) — Pattern F ─
  'j_m4_154': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_descent', x: 0.3, y: 0.55, icon: '⬇️', label: 'Into the Earth', labelAr: 'إلى التراب',
        fragment: 'In the depths of Wednesday night, Ali, Abbas, al-Fadl, and Qutham lowered the Prophet\'s body into the lahd grave dug into the floor of Aisha\'s chamber. They placed him on his right side, facing the qiblah. Unbaked bricks sealed the niche. Earth was placed over the grave and leveled with the floor. The Prophet \uFDFA was buried in the same room where he had lived, prayed, and died. The most important man in history was given the simplest burial.',
        fragmentAr: 'في جوف ليلة الأربعاء أنزل عليّ والعباس والفضل وقُثم جثمان النبيّ \uFDFA في اللحد المحفور في أرض حجرة عائشة. وضعوه على جنبه الأيمن مستقبلًا القبلة. أُغلق اللحد بلَبِنٍ غير مشوي. وُضع التراب فوق القبر وسُوّي مع الأرضية. النبيّ \uFDFA دُفن في الغرفة ذاتها التي عاش فيها وصلّى ومات. أهمّ إنسان في التاريخ مُنح أبسط دفن.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37; Ibn Hisham',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37; Ibn Hisham'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_simplicity', x: 0.7, y: 0.55, icon: '🧱', label: 'No Tomb, No Monument', labelAr: 'لا ضريح ولا نصب',
        fragment: 'There was no coffin. No elaborate tomb. No monument. The grave was at floor level, sealed with unbaked bricks and covered with earth. The Prophet \uFDFA had specifically warned against building structures over graves or turning them into places of worship. His burial embodied that teaching: a man who changed the world was returned to the earth as simply as the poorest person in Medina. The message was the legacy, not the grave.',
        fragmentAr: 'لا تابوت ولا ضريح فاخر ولا نصب. القبر بمستوى الأرض مسدودٌ بلَبِنٍ ومغطّى بالتراب. حذّر النبيّ \uFDFA تحديدًا من البناء فوق القبور أو اتّخاذها مساجد. دفنه جسّد ذلك التعليم: رجلٌ غيّر العالم أُعيد إلى الأرض ببساطة أفقر إنسان في المدينة. الرسالة هي الإرث لا القبر.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #1330; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #1330; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'Abu Bakr and Umar رضي الله عنهما were later buried beside the Prophet \uFDFA in the same chamber, fulfilling a hope Abu Bakr had expressed. Today, the three graves lie beneath the Green Dome of the Prophet\'s Mosque in Medina — the most visited grave site in the Islamic world.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'أبو بكر وعمر رضي الله عنهما دُفنا لاحقًا بجانب النبيّ \uFDFA في الحجرة ذاتها محقّقَين أمنية أبداها أبو بكر. اليوم القبور الثلاثة تحت القبّة الخضراء في المسجد النبوي بالمدينة — أكثر مواقع القبور زيارةً في العالم الإسلامي.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'aisha_s_chamber', x: 0.3, y: 0.32, icon: '🏠', label: 'She Lived with His Grave', labelAr: 'عاشت مع قبره',
        fragment: 'Aisha رضي الله عنها continued to live in the same chamber where the Prophet \uFDFA was buried. The grave was in one part of the room; she slept in another. She said: "I used to enter the room freely when it was only the Prophet \uFDFA and my father buried there. But when Umar was buried, I never entered without covering myself, out of modesty." She lived alongside these graves for nearly fifty years until her own death.',
        fragmentAr: 'واصلت عائشة رضي الله عنها العيش في الحجرة ذاتها التي دُفن فيها النبيّ \uFDFA. القبر في جزء والنوم في آخر. قالت: "كنت أدخل البيت الذي فيه النبيّ \uFDFA وأبي وأنا واضعة ثوبي. فلمّا دُفن عمر فما دخلته إلّا وأنا مشدودة عليّ ثيابي حياءً من عمر." عاشت بجوار هذه القبور نحو خمسين سنة حتّى وفاتها.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Musnad Ahmad; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Musnad Ahmad; Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_new_morning', x: 0.7, y: 0.32, icon: '🌅', label: 'The First Day Without Him', labelAr: 'أوّل يوم بدونه',
        fragment: 'When dawn came on Thursday, Medina woke to its first morning without the Prophet \uFDFA in twenty-three years of prophethood and ten years of residence. The adhan still sounded. The prayer still began. Abu Bakr still led. The Quran was still recited. But the man who had received it, taught it, and lived it was now beneath the floor of a small room. The community he built would carry his message to every corner of the earth. But first, they had to learn to walk without him.',
        fragmentAr: 'حين طلع الفجر يوم الخميس استيقظت المدينة على أوّل صباح بدون النبيّ \uFDFA في ثلاث وعشرين سنة من النبوّة وعشر سنوات من الإقامة. الأذان لا يزال يرتفع. الصلاة لا تزال تبدأ. أبو بكر لا يزال يؤمّ. القرآن لا يزال يُتلى. لكنّ الرجل الذي تلقّاه وعلّمه وعاشه أصبح تحت أرضية غرفةٍ صغيرة. المجتمع الذي بناه سيحمل رسالته إلى كلّ زاوية في الأرض. لكن أوّلًا يجب أن يتعلّموا المشي بدونه.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 37',
        didYouKnow: 'The Prophet \uFDFA was buried in three white Yemeni garments, without a shirt or turban. No silk. No gold. No royal burial. He was wrapped the same way any Muslim is wrapped: simple white cloth, returned to the earth as he came from it. The king of Arabia was buried as a servant of Allah.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'كُفّن النبيّ \uFDFA في ثلاثة أثوابٍ يمانية بيض بلا قميص ولا عمامة. لا حرير ولا ذهب ولا دفن ملكي. لُفّ كما يُلفّ أيّ مسلم: قماش أبيض بسيط أُعيد إلى الأرض كما جاء منها. ملك الجزيرة دُفن كعبدٍ لله.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.78), Offset(0.4, 0.65), Offset(0.3, 0.55), Offset(0.5, 0.55), Offset(0.7, 0.55), Offset(0.6, 0.42), Offset(0.3, 0.32), Offset(0.5, 0.32), Offset(0.7, 0.32)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 155: You Are the Rawi (0 CE) — Pattern C ─
  'j_m4_155': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_scroll_is_full', x: 0.5, y: 0.6, icon: '📜', label: 'Every Page Written', labelAr: 'كلّ صفحة مكتوبة',
        fragment: 'You have walked through 155 moments of the Prophetic era. From the idols of the Ka\'bah to the graves of al-Baqi. From the first "Read" in Cave Hira to the last "Rather the highest companions" in Aisha\'s chamber. You carried the scroll. You discovered the hotspots. You answered the verdicts. You learned the dhikr. You were the Rawi — the narrator in the chain. And now the scroll is full.',
        fragmentAr: 'سلكتَ 155 لحظة من العصر النبوي. من أصنام الكعبة إلى قبور البقيع. من أوّل "اقرأ" في غار حراء إلى آخر "بل الرفيق الأعلى" في حجرة عائشة. حملتَ المخطوطة. اكتشفتَ المحطّات. أجبتَ الأحكام. تعلّمتَ الذكر. كنتَ الراوي — الراوي في السلسلة. والآن المخطوطة ممتلئة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: '',
        sourceRefAr: ''),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_chain_continues', x: 0.22, y: 0.42, icon: '🔗', label: 'You Are a Link', labelAr: 'أنتَ حلقة',
        fragment: 'In Islamic tradition, knowledge passes through chains of transmission — isnad. A teacher heard from a teacher who heard from a teacher, all the way back to the Prophet \uFDFA himself. You are now part of that chain. Not because you read a book, but because you walked the path. You stood where they stood. You heard what they heard. You witnessed what they witnessed. The chain does not end with you. It continues through you.',
        fragmentAr: 'في التقليد الإسلامي ينتقل العلم عبر سلاسل الإسناد. معلّم سمع من معلّم سمع من معلّم حتّى النبيّ \uFDFA نفسه. أنتَ الآن جزء من تلك السلسلة. لا لأنّك قرأت كتابًا بل لأنّك سلكتَ الطريق. وقفتَ حيث وقفوا. سمعتَ ما سمعوا. شهدتَ ما شهدوا. السلسلة لا تنتهي بك. تستمرّ من خلالك.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: '',
        sourceRefAr: ''),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_story_lives', x: 0.5, y: 0.25, icon: '✨', label: 'Tell It Forward', labelAr: 'انقلها إلى الأمام',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA said: "Convey from me, even if it is one verse." You now carry 155 events, hundreds of verses, dozens of hadith, and a lifetime of lessons. The question is not whether you learned — you did. The question is what you do with what you learned. A Rawi does not keep the story. A Rawi passes it on. To your children. To your friends. To your community. To anyone who will listen.',
        fragmentAr: 'قال النبيّ \uFDFA: "بلّغوا عنّي ولو آية." تحمل الآن 155 حدثًا ومئات الآيات وعشرات الأحاديث وعمرًا من الدروس. السؤال ليس هل تعلّمت — بل تعلّمت. السؤال ماذا تفعل بما تعلّمت. الراوي لا يحتفظ بالقصة. الراوي ينقلها. لأطفالك. لأصدقائك. لمجتمعك. لكلّ من يسمع.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3461',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3461'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'you_are_the_rawi', x: 0.78, y: 0.42, icon: '🌟', label: 'The Journey Continues', labelAr: 'الرحلة تستمرّ',
        fragment: 'The scroll is full, but your journey has only begun. The Seerah is not a history lesson. It is a living map. Every event you explored contains a decision you will face in your own life. Every hotspot holds a lesson that applies today. Every dhikr is a door to Allah that never closes. You are not just the reader of this story. You are the next narrator. You are the Rawi. And the story — by the mercy of Allah — never ends.',
        fragmentAr: 'المخطوطة ممتلئة لكنّ رحلتك بدأت للتوّ. السيرة ليست درس تاريخ. إنّها خريطة حيّة. كلّ حدثٍ استكشفته يحمل قرارًا ستواجهه في حياتك. كلّ محطّة تحمل درسًا ينطبق اليوم. كلّ ذكر بابٌ إلى الله لا يُغلق. أنتَ لست مجرّد قارئ لهذه القصة. أنتَ الراوي التالي. أنتَ الراوي. والقصة — برحمة الله — لا تنتهي.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: '',
        sourceRefAr: ''),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.78), Offset(0.5, 0.72), Offset(0.5, 0.6), Offset(0.36, 0.52), Offset(0.22, 0.42), Offset(0.28, 0.32), Offset(0.5, 0.25), Offset(0.72, 0.32), Offset(0.78, 0.42)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ⚠️ NEEDS REVIEW — Event #37 (see RAWI_BLOCKED_EVENTS.md)
  // ── Event 37: Mus'ab ibn Umayr Sent to Medina (620 CE) — Pattern A ─
  'j_m1_037': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_choice', x: 0.5, y: 0.62, icon: '📜', label: 'The First Ambassador', labelAr: 'أوّل سفير',
        fragment: 'After the First Pledge of Aqabah, the new Muslims of Yathrib asked the Prophet \uFDFA to send someone to teach them the Quran and the basics of Islam. The Prophet \uFDFA chose Mus\'ab ibn Umayr رضي الله عنه. Mus\'ab was young, eloquent, handsome, and had been raised in luxury. He had given up everything for Islam — his wealth, his family\'s approval, his comfort. The Prophet \uFDFA did not send a warrior. He sent a teacher.',
        fragmentAr: 'بعد بيعة العقبة الأولى طلب مسلمو يثرب الجدد من النبيّ \uFDFA أن يرسل إليهم من يعلّمهم القرآن وأساسيات الإسلام. فاختار النبيّ \uFDFA مصعب بن عمير رضي الله عنه. كان مصعبٌ شابًّا فصيحًا وسيمًا نشأ في ترف. تخلّى عن كلّ شيء من أجل الإسلام: ثروته ورضا عائلته وراحته. لم يرسل النبيّ \uFDFA محاربًا بل أرسل معلّمًا.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 14 | Ibn Hisham',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 14 | Ibn Hisham'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'teaching_yathrib', x: 0.25, y: 0.44, icon: '📚', label: 'House by House', labelAr: 'بيتًا بيتًا',
        fragment: 'Mus\'ab stayed in the house of As\'ad ibn Zurarah رضي الله عنه, one of the first Medinan converts. From there, he taught the Quran to individuals and small groups. He visited homes, recited to families, answered questions about prayer and faith. His approach was gentle and wise. He did not argue or confront. He recited the Quran and let its beauty do the work. Within months, Islam had entered nearly every household in Yathrib.',
        fragmentAr: 'نزل مصعب في بيت أسعد بن زرارة رضي الله عنه أحد أوائل المسلمين في المدينة. ومن هناك علّم القرآن للأفراد والمجموعات الصغيرة. زار البيوت وتلا للعائلات وأجاب أسئلة عن الصلاة والإيمان. كان أسلوبه لطيفًا وحكيمًا. لم يجادل ولم يواجه. تلا القرآن وترك جماله يعمل. خلال أشهر دخل الإسلام كلّ بيتٍ تقريبًا في يثرب.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 14 | Ibn Hisham',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 14 | Ibn Hisham',
        didYouKnow: 'Mus\'ab\'s mother was one of the wealthiest women in Mecca. When he accepted Islam, she cut off his food, his clothing, and his inheritance. He went from wearing the finest silk in Mecca to wearing patched garments in Medina. The companions said that when they saw Mus\'ab, they would lower their eyes out of compassion for how much he had sacrificed.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'كانت أمّ مصعب من أغنى نساء مكة. حين أسلم قطعت عنه الطعام والكساء والميراث. انتقل من أفخر حرير مكة إلى ثيابٍ مرقّعة في المدينة. قال الصحابة إنّهم حين يرون مصعبًا يغضّون أبصارهم شفقةً ممّا ضحّى.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'usayd_sad', x: 0.72, y: 0.42, icon: '⚔️', label: 'Two Chiefs Convert', labelAr: 'زعيمان يُسلمان',
        fragment: 'Usayd ibn Hudayr, a chief of the Aws tribe, came to confront Mus\'ab with a spear, furious that a Meccan was converting his people. Mus\'ab said calmly: "Will you sit and listen? If you like what you hear, accept it. If not, we will stop." Usayd sat, listened to the Quran, and his face changed. He said: "How beautiful this is!" and accepted Islam. He then sent Sa\'d ibn Mu\'adh, the overall chief of the Aws. Sa\'d came angry, listened, and he too accepted Islam.',
        fragmentAr: 'جاء أسيد بن حضير زعيم الأوس لمواجهة مصعب بحربةٍ غاضبًا أنّ مكّيًّا يحوّل قومه. قال مصعب بهدوء: "أوَتجلس فتسمع؟ فإن رضيت أمرًا قبلته وإن كرهته كُفَّ عنك." جلس أسيد واستمع إلى القرآن فتغيّر وجهه. قال: "ما أحسن هذا وأجمله!" وأسلم. ثمّ أرسل سعد بن معاذ زعيم الأوس الأكبر. جاء سعد غاضبًا واستمع وأسلم هو أيضًا.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 14 | Ibn Hisham',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 14 | Ibn Hisham'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'city_ready', x: 0.5, y: 0.28, icon: '🏙️', label: 'Medina Prepared', labelAr: 'المدينة مستعدّة',
        fragment: 'By the time Mus\'ab returned to Mecca for the next Hajj season, he brought extraordinary news: Islam had spread to virtually every household in Yathrib. Both the Aws and Khazraj tribes had significant Muslim populations. The groundwork for the Hijrah had been laid — not by an army or a political alliance, but by one young teacher with a Quran. Mus\'ab\'s mission to Yathrib was the single most successful act of da\'wah in the entire Seerah.',
        fragmentAr: 'بحلول عودة مصعب إلى مكة في موسم الحجّ التالي حمل خبرًا استثنائيًّا: انتشر الإسلام في كلّ بيتٍ تقريبًا في يثرب. كلتا قبيلتي الأوس والخزرج تضمّ أعدادًا كبيرة من المسلمين. أُرسيت قواعد الهجرة لا بجيشٍ ولا بتحالف سياسي بل بمعلّمٍ شابّ واحد يحمل القرآن. كانت مهمّة مصعب في يثرب أنجح عمل دعويّ في السيرة كلّها.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 14 | Ibn Hisham',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 14 | Ibn Hisham',
        didYouKnow: 'Mus\'ab was later martyred at Uhud. He was the flag-bearer of the Muslim army. When his right hand was cut, he held the flag with his left. When his left was cut, he held it with his stumps. He died clutching the banner. When the companions came to shroud him, his garment was so short that it could not cover both his head and feet. The Prophet \uFDFA said: "Cover his head and put grass on his feet."',
        didYouKnowAr: 'استُشهد مصعب لاحقًا في أُحد. كان حامل راية الجيش المسلم. حين قُطعت يمناه حمل الراية بيسراه. حين قُطعت يسراه حملها بعضديه. مات يعانق الراية. حين جاء الصحابة لتكفينه كان ثوبه قصيرًا لا يغطّي رأسه وقدميه. فقال النبيّ \uFDFA: "غطّوا رأسه واجعلوا على رجليه الإذخر."'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.5, 0.8), Offset(0.51, 0.72), Offset(0.5, 0.62), Offset(0.38, 0.54), Offset(0.25, 0.44), Offset(0.45, 0.42), Offset(0.72, 0.42), Offset(0.63, 0.36), Offset(0.5, 0.28)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ── Event 45: Masjid Quba — First Mosque in Islam (622 CE) — Pattern B ─
  'j_m1_045': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'first_foundation', x: 0.22, y: 0.52, icon: '🧱', label: 'Before Anything Else', labelAr: 'قبل أيّ شيء آخر',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA stayed in Quba for fourteen days after arriving from the Hijrah. The very first thing he did was lay the foundation of a mosque. Not a government building. Not a fortress. Not a marketplace. A place of prayer. This act established the principle that would define the Muslim community forever: wherever Muslims settle, the first structure is the mosque. It is the center of worship, education, law, and community life.',
        fragmentAr: 'أقام النبيّ \uFDFA في قباء أربعة عشر يومًا بعد وصوله من الهجرة. أوّل ما فعله وضع أساس مسجد. لا مبنى حكومة ولا حصن ولا سوق. مكان صلاة. هذا الفعل أرسى المبدأ الذي سيعرّف المجتمع المسلم إلى الأبد: حيثما حلّ المسلمون يكون أوّل بناء المسجد. مركز العبادة والتعليم والقانون والحياة المجتمعية.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16 | Sahih al-Bukhari #3906',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16 | Sahih al-Bukhari #3906'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'prophet_builds', x: 0.42, y: 0.46, icon: '🏗️', label: 'His Own Hands', labelAr: 'بيديه',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA placed the first stone of Masjid Quba with his own hands. Then Abu Bakr placed a stone. Then Umar. Then the companions joined in. The Prophet \uFDFA carried stones alongside everyone else, refusing to sit while others worked. The mosque was simple: stone walls, palm trunk pillars, a roof of palm leaves. No decoration. No luxury. Just a clean space for prayer. The most important building in the new Muslim state cost nothing but labor and faith.',
        fragmentAr: 'وضع النبيّ \uFDFA الحجر الأوّل لمسجد قباء بيديه. ثمّ وضع أبو بكر حجرًا. ثمّ عمر. ثمّ انضمّ الصحابة. حمل النبيّ \uFDFA الحجارة مع الجميع رافضًا الجلوس بينما يعمل الآخرون. كان المسجد بسيطًا: جدران حجرية وأعمدة من جذوع النخل وسقف من سعف النخل. لا زخرفة ولا بذخ. مساحة نظيفة للصلاة فحسب. أهمّ بناء في الدولة الإسلامية الجديدة لم يكلّف إلّا الجهد والإيمان.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16',
        didYouKnow: 'The Quran itself praised Masjid Quba: "A mosque founded on righteousness from the first day is more worthy for you to stand in" (9:108). This verse, revealed years later during the Tabuk expedition in connection with Masjid al-Dirar, is understood by many scholars to refer to Masjid Quba. The first mosque was also the Quran\'s standard for what a mosque should be.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'مدح القرآن نفسه مسجد قباء: ﴿لَمَسْجِدٌ أُسِّسَ عَلَى التَّقْوَىٰ مِنْ أَوَّلِ يَوْمٍ أَحَقُّ أَنْ تَقُومَ فِيهِ﴾. هذه الآية التي نزلت بعد سنوات أثناء حملة تبوك بشأن مسجد الضرار يفهمها كثيرٌ من العلماء إشارةً إلى مسجد قباء. المسجد الأوّل كان أيضًا معيار القرآن لما يجب أن يكونه المسجد.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_reward', x: 0.62, y: 0.4, icon: '✨', label: 'Equal to an Umrah', labelAr: 'كأجر عمرة',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA used to visit Masjid Quba every Saturday, sometimes walking and sometimes riding. He said: "Whoever purifies himself at home, then comes to Masjid Quba and prays there, he will have the reward of an Umrah." This hadith elevated Masjid Quba above every other mosque except the three sacred mosques (Mecca, Medina, Al-Aqsa). The first mosque built in Islam was given a permanent place of honor.',
        fragmentAr: 'كان النبيّ \uFDFA يزور مسجد قباء كلّ سبت ماشيًا أحيانًا وراكبًا أحيانًا. وقال: "من تطهّر في بيته ثمّ أتى مسجد قباء فصلّى فيه كان له كأجر عمرة." هذا الحديث رفع مسجد قباء فوق كلّ مسجد آخر إلّا المساجد الثلاثة المقدّسة (مكة والمدينة والأقصى). المسجد الأوّل في الإسلام مُنح مكانة شرفٍ دائمة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sunan al-Nasa\'i #699 (Sahih) | Sahih Muslim #1399',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sunan al-Nasa\'i #699 (Sahih) | Sahih Muslim #1399'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_principle', x: 0.8, y: 0.34, icon: '🕌', label: 'The Mosque as Foundation', labelAr: 'المسجد كأساس',
        fragment: 'Masjid Quba established the blueprint for every Muslim community that would ever exist: the mosque comes first. Before the state, before the economy, before the military. It is where the community gathers, where disputes are resolved, where knowledge is shared, where the poor are fed, and where the hearts are connected to Allah. Every mosque on earth — from Istanbul to Jakarta to New York — traces its concept back to a few stones laid by a fugitive prophet in a village called Quba.',
        fragmentAr: 'أرسى مسجد قباء المخطّط لكلّ مجتمع مسلم سيوجد: المسجد أوّلًا. قبل الدولة وقبل الاقتصاد وقبل الجيش. حيث يجتمع المجتمع وتُحلّ النزاعات ويُشارك العلم ويُطعم الفقير وتتّصل القلوب بالله. كلّ مسجد على الأرض من إسطنبول إلى جاكرتا إلى نيويورك يعود مفهومه إلى حجارةٍ وضعها نبيٌّ طريد في قريةٍ تُسمّى قباء.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16',
        didYouKnow: 'Masjid Quba still stands today, rebuilt and expanded multiple times over fourteen centuries. It is the second most visited mosque in Medina after the Prophet\'s Mosque. Millions of pilgrims visit it annually, following the Prophet\'s Sunnah of praying there for the reward of an Umrah.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'لا يزال مسجد قباء قائمًا اليوم أُعيد بناؤه ووُسّع مرّات عدّة عبر أربعة عشر قرنًا. ثاني أكثر مسجد زيارةً في المدينة بعد المسجد النبوي. يزوره ملايين الحجّاج سنويًّا اتّباعًا لسنّة النبيّ في الصلاة فيه لأجر عمرة.'),
    ],
    pathWaypoints: const [Offset(0.12, 0.58), Offset(0.17, 0.55), Offset(0.22, 0.52), Offset(0.32, 0.49), Offset(0.42, 0.46), Offset(0.52, 0.43), Offset(0.62, 0.4), Offset(0.72, 0.37), Offset(0.8, 0.34)],
    skyGradient: const [Color(0xFF000000), Color(0xFF000000)],
    particleType: ParticleType.dust, particleCount: 20, particleColor: const Color(0x40C9A84C), showGrain: true,
  ),

  // ⚠️ NEEDS REVIEW — Event #46 (see RAWI_BLOCKED_EVENTS.md)
  // ── Event 46: The First Friday Prayer (622 CE) — Pattern D ─
  'j_m1_046': SceneConfig(
    hubLayers: _meccaHubLayers,
    groundLayers: const [],
    hotspots: const [
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_stop', x: 0.5, y: 0.5, icon: '🕌', label: 'Friday in a Valley', labelAr: 'جمعة في وادٍ',
        fragment: 'On his way from Quba to Yathrib, the Prophet \uFDFA stopped in the valley of Banu Salim ibn Awf when Friday midday arrived. He led the first Jumu\'ah (Friday congregational prayer) in Islam. There was no mosque here — just open ground. The khutbah was delivered under the sky. The prayer was performed on the earth. The institution of Friday prayer did not begin in a grand building. It began on a road, during a journey, because the time for prayer does not wait for architecture.',
        fragmentAr: 'في طريقه من قباء إلى يثرب توقّف النبيّ \uFDFA في وادي بني سالم بن عوف حين حلّ ظهر الجمعة. أمّ أوّل صلاة جمعة في الإسلام. لم يكن هناك مسجد بل أرضٌ مفتوحة. أُلقيت الخطبة تحت السماء. أُدّيت الصلاة على الأرض. مؤسسة صلاة الجمعة لم تبدأ في مبنىً فخم بل على طريقٍ أثناء رحلة لأنّ وقت الصلاة لا ينتظر العمارة.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16 | Ibn Hisham',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16 | Ibn Hisham'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_khutbah', x: 0.25, y: 0.4, icon: '🎤', label: 'The First Sermon', labelAr: 'الخطبة الأولى',
        fragment: 'The Prophet \uFDFA delivered the first Friday sermon (khutbah) to approximately one hundred Muslims. He praised Allah, reminded them of their duty to worship Him alone, and spoke of the accountability of the Day of Judgment. He encouraged them to prepare for the afterlife by doing good deeds. The khutbah was brief, direct, and focused. It established the template: Friday sermons are reminders, not lectures. They connect the week\'s work to the soul\'s purpose.',
        fragmentAr: 'ألقى النبيّ \uFDFA أوّل خطبة جمعة أمام نحو مئة مسلم. حمد الله وذكّرهم بواجب عبادته وحده وتحدّث عن حساب يوم القيامة. حثّهم على الاستعداد للآخرة بالعمل الصالح. كانت الخطبة موجزة ومباشرة ومركّزة. أرست النموذج: خطب الجمعة تذكيرات لا محاضرات. تربط عمل الأسبوع بغاية الروح.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16 | Ibn Hisham',
        sourceRefAr: 'Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16 | Ibn Hisham',
        didYouKnow: 'The location where the first Jumu\'ah was held later became known as Masjid al-Jumu\'ah (the Friday Mosque). It still stands today in Medina, between Quba and the Prophet\'s Mosque, marking the exact spot where the institution of Friday prayer was born.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'الموقع الذي أُقيمت فيه أوّل جمعة عُرف لاحقًا بمسجد الجمعة. لا يزال قائمًا في المدينة بين قباء والمسجد النبوي يحدّد المكان الذي وُلدت فيه مؤسسة صلاة الجمعة.'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'the_institution', x: 0.75, y: 0.4, icon: '📅', label: 'Every Friday, Forever', labelAr: 'كلّ جمعة إلى الأبد',
        fragment: 'The Quran would later command: "O you who believe, when the call to prayer is made on Friday, hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade" (62:9). Friday became the weekly gathering of the Muslim community worldwide. Every week, in every city, in every mosque, Muslims stop their work and gather. They hear a khutbah. They pray together. They renew their connection. This weekly rhythm, practiced by over a billion people, began with one stop in a valley.',
        fragmentAr: 'سيأمر القرآن لاحقًا: ﴿يَا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِذَا نُودِيَ لِلصَّلَاةِ مِنْ يَوْمِ الْجُمُعَةِ فَاسْعَوْا إِلَىٰ ذِكْرِ اللَّهِ وَذَرُوا الْبَيْعَ﴾. أصبحت الجمعة التجمّع الأسبوعي للمجتمع المسلم عالميًّا. كلّ أسبوع في كلّ مدينة في كلّ مسجد يتوقّف المسلمون عن عملهم ويجتمعون. يسمعون خطبة. يصلّون معًا. يجدّدون صلتهم. هذا الإيقاع الأسبوعي الذي يمارسه أكثر من مليار شخص بدأ بتوقّف واحد في وادٍ.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Quran 62:9 | Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16',
        sourceRefAr: 'Quran 62:9 | Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16'),
      SceneHotspot(id: 'continuing_journey', x: 0.5, y: 0.28, icon: '🐪', label: 'Onward to Yathrib', labelAr: 'إلى يثرب',
        fragment: 'After the Friday prayer, the Prophet \uFDFA remounted al-Qaswa and continued toward Yathrib. Everyone wanted to host him. Clan after clan reached for his camel\'s reins, saying: "Stay with us, O Messenger of Allah! We have numbers and provisions and defense." The Prophet \uFDFA said: "Let the camel go, for she is commanded." Al-Qaswa walked through the streets of Yathrib and knelt in an open space owned by two orphans. There, the Prophet\'s Mosque would be built.',
        fragmentAr: 'بعد صلاة الجمعة امتطى النبيّ \uFDFA القصواء ومضى نحو يثرب. أراد الجميع استضافته. بطنٌ تلو بطن يمسك بزمام ناقته قائلًا: "أقم عندنا يا رسول الله! عندنا العدد والعُدّة والمنعة." فقال النبيّ \uFDFA: "دعوها فإنّها مأمورة." سارت القصواء في شوارع يثرب وبركت في ساحةٍ مفتوحة يملكها يتيمان. هناك سيُبنى المسجد النبوي.',
        sfxPath: 'assets/audio/sfx_kaabah_wind.wav',
        sourceRef: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3906 | Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16',
        sourceRefAr: 'Sahih al-Bukhari #3906 | Al-Raheeq Al-Makhtum Ch. 16',
        didYouKnow: 'The two orphans who owned the land where al-Qaswa knelt were Sahl and Suhayl, sons of Amr. The Prophet \uFDFA insisted on paying them for the land, refusing to take it as a gift. He paid ten dinars. The most important piece of real estate in Islamic history was purchased at fair market value from two orphans.',
        didYouKnowAr: 'اليتيمان اللذان يملكان الأرض حيث بركت القصواء هما سهل وسهيل ابنا عمرو. أصرّ النبيّ \uFDFA على الدفع لهما رافضًا أخذها هدية. دفع عشرة دنانير. أهمّ قطعة عقار في التاريخ الإسلامي شُريت بسعر السوق العادل من يتيمين.'),
